  <!-- Raw Embedded Quiz Data -->
  <script>
    const RAW_QUIZ_DATA = {"metadata":{"title":"Philippine CPA Review - Auditing Theory Question Bank (11 Supernovas)","sets":[{"id":"AT-100","name":"AT-100 (100 Questions - Supernovas Clean)","count":100},{"id":"AT-300","name":"AT-300 (300 Questions - Supernovas Clean)","count":300}],"passingThresholdPercent":75,"totalQuestions":400},"questions":{"AT-100":[{"id":"AT-100-1","set":"AT-100","num":1,"question":"An auditor compares 2010 revenues and expenses with those of the prior\nyear and investigates all changes exceeding 10%. By this procedure the\nauditor would be most likely to learn that","options":{"A":"An increase in property tax rates has not been recognized in the\nclient's accrual","B":"The 2010 provision for uncollectible accounts is inadequate, because\nof worsening economic conditions","C":"Fourth quarter payroll taxes were not paid","D":"The client changed its capitalization policy for small tools in 2010."},"correctAnswer":"D","topic":"Analytical Procedures","standard":"PSA 520 (Analytical Procedures)","rationale":"Comparing current year revenues and expenses with prior period amounts and investigating fluctuations exceeding a defined threshold (10%) is an analytical procedure. A change in the client's capitalization policy for small tools (e.g., directly expensing items previously capitalized) creates an immediate, noticeable change in the small tools expense account exceeding the 10% threshold.","distractorNotes":"Unrecognized accruals, inadequate allowances for uncollectibles, or unpaid payroll taxes typically require tests of details and cutoff testing, and may not produce a single obvious >10% variance on comparative income statement accounts."},{"id":"AT-100-2","set":"AT-100","num":2,"question":"An abnormal fluctuation in gross profit that might suggest the need\nfor extended audit procedures for sales and inventories would most likely\nbe identified in the planning phase of the audit by the use of","options":{"A":"tests of transactions and balances","B":"a preliminary review of internal control","C":"specialized audit programs","D":"analytical procedures."},"correctAnswer":"D","topic":"Analytical Procedures","standard":"PSA 520 (Analytical Procedures)","rationale":"Analytical procedures performed during the planning phase evaluate financial information by studying relationships among data. Investigating abnormal fluctuations in gross profit helps identify unexpected relationships that signal risk of material misstatement in revenues and inventory, directing the nature, timing, and extent of extended substantive procedures.","distractorNotes":"Tests of transactions and balances are substantive procedures performed during fieldwork, while internal control reviews and specialized audit programs do not directly uncover margin fluctuations."},{"id":"AT-100-3","set":"AT-100","num":3,"question":"Which of the following events occurring after the issuance of an\nauditor‘s report most likely would cause the auditor to make further\ninquiries about the previously issued financial statements","options":{"A":"A technological development that could affect the entity's future -\nability to continue as a going concern","B":"The discovery of information regarding a contingency that existed\nbefore the financial statements were issued","C":"The entity's sale of a subsidiary that accounts for 30% of the entity's\nconsolidated sales","D":"The final resolution of a lawsuit which is adequately explained in a\nseparate paragraph of the auditor's report."},"correctAnswer":"B","topic":"Subsequent Events & Discovery of Facts","standard":"PSA 560 - Subsequent Events","rationale":"Under PSA 560, after the financial statements are issued, the auditor has no general obligation to perform further procedures. However, if the auditor becomes aware of a fact that existed at the date of the report which, if known, might have altered the report (such as discovery of an existing contingency), the auditor must discuss the matter with management and determine whether the statements need revision.","distractorNotes":"Events occurring after issuance that relate to new conditions (such as subsequent technological changes or new subsidiary sales) do not require reopening prior statements."},{"id":"AT-100-4","set":"AT-100","num":4,"question":"Which of the following statements concerning litigation, claims and\nassessments which were extracted from a letter from a client's lawyer\nis most likely to cause the auditor to request clarification","options":{"A":"“I\nbelieve that the possible liability to the company is nominal in amount.”","B":"\"I believe that the action can be settled for less than the damages\nclaimed.\"","C":"“I believe that the plaintiff's case against the company is without\nmerit.”","D":"“I believe that the company will be able to defend this action\nsuccessfully.”"},"correctAnswer":"B","topic":"Audit Principles & Evidence","standard":"Philippine Standards on Auditing (PSA)","rationale":"Under auditing standards and professional practice, Option B accurately reflects the applicable requirement. The auditor gathers sufficient appropriate evidence to provide reasonable assurance regarding the relevant management assertions and financial statement disclosures.","distractorNotes":"The other choices are either inconsistent with Philippine auditing standards or represent incorrect procedures for this engagement stage."},{"id":"AT-100-5","set":"AT-100","num":5,"question":"Road, CPA, believes there is \"substantial doubt about the ability of\nKennon Company to continue as a going concern for a reasonable period\nof time. In evaluating Kennon's plan for dealing with the adverse effects\nof future conditions and events, Rood most likely would consider, as a\nmitigating factor, Kennon's plans to","options":{"A":"Make the credit terms for sales on account more lenient","B":"strengthen internal controls over cash disbursements","C":"purchase the production facilities currently being leased from a\nrelated party","D":"postpone those expenditures for research and development projects."},"correctAnswer":"D","topic":"Going Concern Assessment","standard":"PSA 570 (Revised) - Going Concern","rationale":"Under PSA 570, when conditions indicate substantial doubt regarding going concern, the auditor evaluates management's plans for future actions. Management plans to postpone or delay discretionary expenditures, such as research and development or capital expenditures, preserve cash flow and mitigate short-term solvency distress.","distractorNotes":"Lenient credit terms, purchasing leased assets, or paying cash dividends worsen liquidity pressure rather than mitigating going-concern doubt."},{"id":"AT-100-6","set":"AT-100","num":6,"question":"The auditor is most likely to discover omitted audit procedures\nduring","options":{"A":"preparation of the management letter","B":"follow-up procedures performed in compliance with generally accepted\nauditing standards","C":"a post engagement review performed as part of the firm's quality\ncontrol inspection program","D":"the final review of the working papers."},"correctAnswer":"C","topic":"Audit Principles & Evidence","standard":"Philippine Standards on Auditing (PSA)","rationale":"Under auditing standards and professional practice, Option C accurately reflects the applicable requirement. The auditor gathers sufficient appropriate evidence to provide reasonable assurance regarding the relevant management assertions and financial statement disclosures.","distractorNotes":"The other choices are either inconsistent with Philippine auditing standards or represent incorrect procedures for this engagement stage."},{"id":"AT-100-7","set":"AT-100","num":7,"question":"Internal auditors are most likely to issue a report on which of the\nfollowing","options":{"A":"Annual financial statement reporting","B":"Internal control","C":"Tax compliance","D":"Quarterly financial statement reporting"},"correctAnswer":"B","topic":"Audit Principles & Evidence","standard":"Philippine Standards on Auditing (PSA)","rationale":"Under auditing standards and professional practice, Option B accurately reflects the applicable requirement. The auditor gathers sufficient appropriate evidence to provide reasonable assurance regarding the relevant management assertions and financial statement disclosures.","distractorNotes":"The other choices are either inconsistent with Philippine auditing standards or represent incorrect procedures for this engagement stage."},{"id":"AT-100-8","set":"AT-100","num":8,"question":"You have been selected to develop an internal auditing department for\nyour company. Your approach would most likely be to hire","options":{"A":"internal auditors each of whom possesses all the skills required to\nhandle all audit assignments","B":"inexperienced personnel and train them the way the company wants them\ntrained","C":"degreed accountants since most audit work is accounting related","D":"internal auditors who collectively have the knowledge and skills\nneeded to complete all internal audit assignments."},"correctAnswer":"D","topic":"Audit Principles & Evidence","standard":"Philippine Standards on Auditing (PSA)","rationale":"Under auditing standards and professional practice, Option D accurately reflects the applicable requirement. The auditor gathers sufficient appropriate evidence to provide reasonable assurance regarding the relevant management assertions and financial statement disclosures.","distractorNotes":"The other choices are either inconsistent with Philippine auditing standards or represent incorrect procedures for this engagement stage."},{"id":"AT-100-9","set":"AT-100","num":9,"question":"In performing an operational audit, internal auditors are most likely\nto focus upon which of the following attributes","options":{"A":"Controls relating to the adequacy of the financial statements","B":"All controls and not just those relating to financial statements","C":"Efficiency of operations","D":"Operations of the business including efficiency, effectiveness,\ninternal control and others."},"correctAnswer":"D","topic":"Audit Sampling (Statistical & Non-Statistical)","standard":"PSA 530 - Audit Sampling","rationale":"PSA 530 defines audit sampling as the application of audit procedures to less than 100% of items within a population of audit relevance such that all sampling units have a chance of selection in order to provide the auditor with a reasonable basis on which to draw conclusions about the entire population.","distractorNotes":"Other options misstate the relationship between sampling risk parameters (alpha/beta risk, tolerable misstatement, and sample size)."},{"id":"AT-100-10","set":"AT-100","num":10,"question":"An operational auditor is most likely to be concerned with whether\no transaction was","options":{"A":"necessary","B":"reasonable","C":"properly approved","D":"properly supported with documentation."},"correctAnswer":"A","topic":"Operational & Internal Auditing","standard":"Philippine Framework for Assurance Engagements","rationale":"Operational auditing is a systematic review of an organization's activities in relation to the efficient and effective use of resources, compliance with operational policies, and achievement of organizational objectives.","distractorNotes":"Financial statement audits focus on fair presentation in conformity with PFRS, whereas operational audits focus on economy, efficiency, and effectiveness."},{"id":"AT-100-11","set":"AT-100","num":11,"question":"An auditor who was engaged to perform an examination of the financial\nstatements of a non-public entity has been asked by the client to refrain\nfrom performing various audit procedures and changed the nature of the\nengagement to a review of financial statements. The client made the\nrequest because of the significant cost of completing the examination.\nThe auditor would most likely","options":{"A":"qualify the auditor's report and refer to the scope limitation","B":"view the request as an indication of possible irregularity","C":"complete the examination in progress","D":"grant the client request"},"correctAnswer":"D","topic":"Review Engagements","standard":"PSRE 2400 - Engagements to Review Financial Statements","rationale":"A review engagement provides moderate (limited) assurance, primarily using inquiry and analytical procedures. It concludes in negative assurance form ('nothing has come to our attention that causes us to believe that the financial statements are not prepared, in all material respects, in accordance with...').","distractorNotes":"A review does not involve assessing internal control, observing inventory, or sending debt confirmations."},{"id":"AT-100-12","set":"AT-100","num":12,"question":"The circumstance most likely to make it impossible for a practitioner\nto issue a review report is when the","options":{"A":"criteria are only available to specified users","B":"subject matter contains a departure from the criteria","C":"company faces a going concern uncertainty","D":"scope of the engagement has been significantly limited"},"correctAnswer":"D","topic":"Audit Principles & Evidence","standard":"Philippine Standards on Auditing (PSA)","rationale":"Under auditing standards and professional practice, Option D accurately reflects the applicable requirement. The auditor gathers sufficient appropriate evidence to provide reasonable assurance regarding the relevant management assertions and financial statement disclosures.","distractorNotes":"The other choices are either inconsistent with Philippine auditing standards or represent incorrect procedures for this engagement stage."},{"id":"AT-100-13","set":"AT-100","num":13,"question":"Accepting an engagement to examine an entity's financial projection\nmost likely would be appropriate if the projection were to be distributed\nto","options":{"A":"all employees who work for the entity","B":"potential stockholders who request a prospectus or a registration\nstatement","C":"a bank with which the entity is negotiating for a loan","D":"all stockholders of record as of the report date"},"correctAnswer":"C","topic":"Audit Principles & Evidence","standard":"Philippine Standards on Auditing (PSA)","rationale":"Under auditing standards and professional practice, Option C accurately reflects the applicable requirement. The auditor gathers sufficient appropriate evidence to provide reasonable assurance regarding the relevant management assertions and financial statement disclosures.","distractorNotes":"The other choices are either inconsistent with Philippine auditing standards or represent incorrect procedures for this engagement stage."},{"id":"AT-100-14","set":"AT-100","num":14,"question":"An auditor will most likely estimate the tolerable failure rate in\norder to","options":{"A":"determine which type of sampling approach to use","B":"calculate the probable control risk","C":"determine the population to be tested","D":"determine the appropriate sample size"},"correctAnswer":"D","topic":"Audit Sampling (Statistical & Non-Statistical)","standard":"PSA 530 - Audit Sampling","rationale":"The tolerable rate of deviation (or failure rate) is the maximum rate set by the auditor for which control risk can still be supported. An auditor estimates this parameter during planning to determine the appropriate sample size necessary to achieve the desired confidence level.","distractorNotes":"Tolerable rate does not determine the sampling approach (statistical vs nonstatistical) nor does it define the population boundaries."},{"id":"AT-100-15","set":"AT-100","num":15,"question":"The product of inherent risk and control risk is assessed as low.\nHow would an auditor with this assessment most likely test depreciation\nexpense","options":{"A":"As a ratio of total assets","B":"As a percent of sales","C":"By re-computing all depreciation figures","D":"By fagging and tracing transactions through the system"},"correctAnswer":"A","topic":"Internal Control & Control Risk","standard":"PSA 315 & PSA 330 (Internal Control & Tests of Controls)","rationale":"Under PSA 315, internal control is designed, implemented, and maintained to provide reasonable assurance regarding reliability of financial reporting, effectiveness and efficiency of operations, and compliance with applicable laws and regulations.","distractorNotes":"Other options reflect management operational targets or clerical procedures that do not directly address internal control objectives."},{"id":"AT-100-16","set":"AT-100","num":16,"question":"What kind of threat to independence most likely occurs when any\nproduct or judgment of a previous assurance engagement or non-assurance\nengagement needs to be re-evaluated in reaching conclusions on the\n\n\n\n\nassurance engagement or when a member of that, assurance team was\npreviously a director or officer of the assurance client, or was an\nemployee in a position to exert direct and significant influence over\nthe subject matter of the assurance engagement","options":{"A":"Self-interest threat","B":"Advocacy threat","C":"Self-review threat","D":"Familiarity threat."},"correctAnswer":"C","topic":"Professional Ethics & Independence","standard":"Philippine Code of Ethics for Professional Accountants","rationale":"A Self-Review Threat occurs when any product or judgment of a previous assurance or non-assurance engagement needs to be re-evaluated by the same firm in reaching conclusions on the current assurance engagement, or when an auditor evaluates their own prior work.","distractorNotes":"Self-interest arises from financial gain; advocacy arises from promoting a client's position; familiarity arises from long or close personal relationships."},{"id":"AT-100-17","set":"AT-100","num":17,"question":"Which of the following is most likely a violation of the Code of\nEthics","options":{"A":"The professional accountant in public practice bills a client a fee,\nlower than what previously has been charged for similar services","B":"The professional accountant makes a representation that specific\nprofessional services in the current or future periods will be performed\nfor a stated fee if it is likely at the time of representation that such\nfee will be substantially increased","C":"The professional accountant in public practice is the one who\ndetermines the appropriate billing rates of each professional staff\nengaged in performing services","D":"The professional accountant agrees to the client's proposal for a\nprofessional fee that is dependent to the number of service hours\nrendered"},"correctAnswer":"B","topic":"Audit Principles & Evidence","standard":"Philippine Standards on Auditing (PSA)","rationale":"Under auditing standards and professional practice, Option B accurately reflects the applicable requirement. The auditor gathers sufficient appropriate evidence to provide reasonable assurance regarding the relevant management assertions and financial statement disclosures.","distractorNotes":"The other choices are either inconsistent with Philippine auditing standards or represent incorrect procedures for this engagement stage."},{"id":"AT-100-18","set":"AT-100","num":18,"question":"Which of the following circumstances would most likely cause an\nauditor to suspect that material fraud exists in a client's financial\nstatements","options":{"A":"Property and equipment items are usually sold at a loss before being\nfully depreciated","B":"Significantly fewer responses to a positive confirmation requests are\nreceived than what is expected","C":"Monthly bank reconciliations usually include several in-transit\nitems","D":"Clerical errors are listed on a CBIS-generated exception report."},"correctAnswer":"B","topic":"Fraud & Error in an Audit","standard":"PSA 240 - The Auditor's Responsibilities Relating to Fraud","rationale":"PSA 240 distinguishes fraud (intentional act by one or more individuals among management, employees, or third parties resulting in a misstatement) from error (unintentional mistake). The primary responsibility for the prevention and detection of fraud rests with management and governance.","distractorNotes":"Other choices misstate auditor responsibilities or fail to recognize the necessity of maintaining professional skepticism."},{"id":"AT-100-19","set":"AT-100","num":19,"question":"When the auditor performs tests of control regarding the policy\ncounting materials received from the supplies, the auditor most likely","options":{"A":"inspect the receiving reports","B":"verify that cash-vouchers are accompanied by receiving reports","C":"inquire with the receiving clerk of whether count of incoming\ndeliveries is being made","D":"observe several times as the receiving section is receiving the\nincoming deliveries."},"correctAnswer":"D","topic":"Internal Control & Control Risk","standard":"PSA 315 & PSA 330 (Internal Control & Tests of Controls)","rationale":"Under PSA 315, internal control is designed, implemented, and maintained to provide reasonable assurance regarding reliability of financial reporting, effectiveness and efficiency of operations, and compliance with applicable laws and regulations.","distractorNotes":"Other options reflect management operational targets or clerical procedures that do not directly address internal control objectives."},{"id":"AT-100-20","set":"AT-100","num":20,"question":"Which of the following accounts would most likely be reviewed by the\nauditor to gain reasonable assurance that additions to the equipment\naccount are not understated","options":{"A":"Repairs and maintenance expense","B":"Depreciation expense","C":"Gain on disposal of equipment","D":"Accounts payable"},"correctAnswer":"A","topic":"Audit Principles & Evidence","standard":"Philippine Standards on Auditing (PSA)","rationale":"Under auditing standards and professional practice, Option A accurately reflects the applicable requirement. The auditor gathers sufficient appropriate evidence to provide reasonable assurance regarding the relevant management assertions and financial statement disclosures.","distractorNotes":"The other choices are either inconsistent with Philippine auditing standards or represent incorrect procedures for this engagement stage."},{"id":"AT-100-21","set":"AT-100","num":21,"question":"An audit of the General Lizard Company, a home appliance manufacturer\ncompany, detects material misapplication of the measurement of the lower\nof cost or market principle. General Lizard's executive and financial\nmanagement will not change the recorded amounts or disclosures to the\nauditor's satisfaction. Such a situation will most likely result in which\ntype of report","options":{"A":"Unqualified","B":"Disclaimer","C":"Qualified or adverse","D":"Negative assurance"},"correctAnswer":"C","topic":"Audit Principles & Evidence","standard":"Philippine Standards on Auditing (PSA)","rationale":"Under auditing standards and professional practice, Option C accurately reflects the applicable requirement. The auditor gathers sufficient appropriate evidence to provide reasonable assurance regarding the relevant management assertions and financial statement disclosures.","distractorNotes":"The other choices are either inconsistent with Philippine auditing standards or represent incorrect procedures for this engagement stage."},{"id":"AT-100-22","set":"AT-100","num":22,"question":"The auditors of White Stained Sheets, Inc. are unable to obtain\nevidence regarding accounts receivable which is a material balance.\nInstead, the auditors are able .to satisfy themselves with other\nalternative procedures relating to the White Stained Sheets audit. Which\nreport will the auditors most likely issue in this situation","options":{"A":"Qualified","B":"Unqualified","C":"Adverse","D":"Disclaimer"},"correctAnswer":"B","topic":"Substantive Testing & Confirmations","standard":"PSA 500, 501 & 505 (Audit Evidence & Confirmations)","rationale":"Under PSA 500 and 505, substantive procedures are designed to detect material misstatements at the assertion level. External confirmations provide highly reliable third-party evidence regarding balances and contract terms.","distractorNotes":"Other options address distinct assertions that require different procedures such as recalculation, observation, or inquiry."},{"id":"AT-100-23","set":"AT-100","num":23,"question":"Which one of the following subsequent events will most likely result\nto an adjustment to the financial statements","options":{"A":"Material change in the amount of settlement, of a lawsuit which had\nbeen estimated at year end","B":"Entry into a significant-new line of products and business","C":"Proceeds received-from a 'related party note payable","D":"Signing of a letter-of-intent by the' client to acquire 55% of another\nentity for stock"},"correctAnswer":"A","topic":"Subsequent Events & Discovery of Facts","standard":"PSA 560 - Subsequent Events","rationale":"Subsequent events providing additional evidence about conditions that existed at the balance sheet date (Type 1 events) require an adjustment to the financial statements. A material change in the settlement of a litigation contingency that existed at year-end provides definitive valuation evidence requiring financial statement adjustment.","distractorNotes":"New business lines, subsequent financing transactions, or letters of intent are non-adjusting Type 2 events that require disclosure only."},{"id":"AT-100-24","set":"AT-100","num":24,"question":"Which one of the following is a key condition indicating doubt\nregarding an entity's ability to continue as a going-concern","options":{"A":"Improvement in key financial ratios","B":"Litigation in the normal course of business","C":"The company‘s auditors were voted out in the most previous\nshareholder's meeting","D":"Inability to make principal and interest payments as they become due"},"correctAnswer":"D","topic":"Audit Principles & Evidence","standard":"Philippine Standards on Auditing (PSA)","rationale":"Under auditing standards and professional practice, Option D accurately reflects the applicable requirement. The auditor gathers sufficient appropriate evidence to provide reasonable assurance regarding the relevant management assertions and financial statement disclosures.","distractorNotes":"The other choices are either inconsistent with Philippine auditing standards or represent incorrect procedures for this engagement stage."},{"id":"AT-100-25","set":"AT-100","num":25,"question":"A successor auditor would most likely make specific inquiries of the\npredecessor auditor regarding","options":{"A":"Specialized accounting principles of the client's industry","B":"The competency of the client's internal audit staff","C":"The uncertainty inherent in applying sampling procedures","D":"Disagreements With management as to auditing procedures."},"correctAnswer":"D","topic":"Audit Principles & Evidence","standard":"Philippine Standards on Auditing (PSA)","rationale":"Under auditing standards and professional practice, Option D accurately reflects the applicable requirement. The auditor gathers sufficient appropriate evidence to provide reasonable assurance regarding the relevant management assertions and financial statement disclosures.","distractorNotes":"The other choices are either inconsistent with Philippine auditing standards or represent incorrect procedures for this engagement stage."},{"id":"AT-100-26","set":"AT-100","num":26,"question":"Which of the following factors most likely would cause an auditor\nnot to accept a new audit engagement","options":{"A":"An inadequate understanding of the entity's internal control\nstructure","B":"The close proximity to the end of the entity's fiscal year","C":"Concluding that the entity's management probably lacks integrity","D":"An inability to perform preliminary, analytical assessing control\nrisk."},"correctAnswer":"C","topic":"Audit Principles & Evidence","standard":"Philippine Standards on Auditing (PSA)","rationale":"Under auditing standards and professional practice, Option C accurately reflects the applicable requirement. The auditor gathers sufficient appropriate evidence to provide reasonable assurance regarding the relevant management assertions and financial statement disclosures.","distractorNotes":"The other choices are either inconsistent with Philippine auditing standards or represent incorrect procedures for this engagement stage."},{"id":"AT-100-27","set":"AT-100","num":27,"question":"Which of the following procedures would an auditor most likely\nperform in planning a financial statement audit","options":{"A":"Inquiring of the client's legal counsel concerning pending,\nlitigation","B":"Comparing the financial statements to anticipated-results","C":"Examining computer generated exception reports to verify the\neffectiveness of internal controls","D":"Searching for unauthorized transactions that may aid in detecting\nunrecorded liabilities"},"correctAnswer":"B","topic":"Audit Principles & Evidence","standard":"Philippine Standards on Auditing (PSA)","rationale":"Under auditing standards and professional practice, Option B accurately reflects the applicable requirement. The auditor gathers sufficient appropriate evidence to provide reasonable assurance regarding the relevant management assertions and financial statement disclosures.","distractorNotes":"The other choices are either inconsistent with Philippine auditing standards or represent incorrect procedures for this engagement stage."},{"id":"AT-100-28","set":"AT-100","num":28,"question":"Inquiries directed towards those charged with governance may most\nlikely","options":{"A":"Relate to their activities concerning the design and effectiveness\nof the entity's internal control and whether management has\nsatisfactorily responded to any findings from these activities","B":"Help the auditor understand the environment in which the financial\nstatements are prepared","C":"Relate to changes in the entity's marketing strategies, sales trends\nor contractual arrangements with its customers","D":"Help the auditor in evaluating the appropriateness of the selection\nand application of certain accounting policies"},"correctAnswer":"B","topic":"Audit Principles & Evidence","standard":"Philippine Standards on Auditing (PSA)","rationale":"Under auditing standards and professional practice, Option B accurately reflects the applicable requirement. The auditor gathers sufficient appropriate evidence to provide reasonable assurance regarding the relevant management assertions and financial statement disclosures.","distractorNotes":"The other choices are either inconsistent with Philippine auditing standards or represent incorrect procedures for this engagement stage."},{"id":"AT-100-29","set":"AT-100","num":29,"question":"In auditing accounts payable, an auditor's procedures most likely\nwill focus primarily on management's assertion of","options":{"A":"Existence or occurrence","B":"Completeness","C":"Presentation and disclosure","D":"Valuation or allocation"},"correctAnswer":"B","topic":"Substantive Testing & Confirmations","standard":"PSA 500, 501 & 505 (Audit Evidence & Confirmations)","rationale":"Under PSA 500 and 505, substantive procedures are designed to detect material misstatements at the assertion level. External confirmations provide highly reliable third-party evidence regarding balances and contract terms.","distractorNotes":"Other options address distinct assertions that require different procedures such as recalculation, observation, or inquiry."},{"id":"AT-100-30","set":"AT-100","num":30,"question":"An auditor is most likely to inspect loan' agreements under which\nan entity's inventories are pledged to support management‘s financial\nstatement assertion of","options":{"A":"Existence or occurrence","B":"Presentation and disclosure","C":"Completeness","D":"Valuation or allocation"},"correctAnswer":"B","topic":"Audit Principles & Evidence","standard":"Philippine Standards on Auditing (PSA)","rationale":"Under auditing standards and professional practice, Option B accurately reflects the applicable requirement. The auditor gathers sufficient appropriate evidence to provide reasonable assurance regarding the relevant management assertions and financial statement disclosures.","distractorNotes":"The other choices are either inconsistent with Philippine auditing standards or represent incorrect procedures for this engagement stage."},{"id":"AT-100-31","set":"AT-100","num":31,"question":"An auditor selected items for test counts while observing a client's\nphysical inventory. The auditor then traced the test counts to the\nclient's inventory listing. This procedure most likely obtained evidence\nconcerning","options":{"A":"Rights and obligations","B":"Existence or occurrence","C":"Completeness","D":"Valuation"},"correctAnswer":"C","topic":"Substantive Testing & Confirmations","standard":"PSA 500, 501 & 505 (Audit Evidence & Confirmations)","rationale":"Under PSA 500 and 505, substantive procedures are designed to detect material misstatements at the assertion level. External confirmations provide highly reliable third-party evidence regarding balances and contract terms.","distractorNotes":"Other options address distinct assertions that require different procedures such as recalculation, observation, or inquiry."},{"id":"AT-100-32","set":"AT-100","num":32,"question":"Confirmation is most likely to be a relevant form of evidence with\nregard to assertions about accounts receivable when the auditor has\nconcern about the receivables","options":{"A":"Valuation","B":"Classification","C":"Existence","D":"Completeness"},"correctAnswer":"C","topic":"Substantive Testing & Confirmations","standard":"PSA 500, 501 & 505 (Audit Evidence & Confirmations)","rationale":"External confirmation directly with customers provides primary substantive evidence regarding the Existence and Rights of recorded accounts receivable balances. Valuation (net realizable value) cannot be verified by confirmation alone because the customer confirms owing the debt, not their financial capacity to pay.","distractorNotes":"Valuation requires examining aging schedules, historical loss rates, and subsequent cash collections, not mere confirmation of the gross balance."},{"id":"AT-100-33","set":"AT-100","num":33,"question":"The auditor most likely performs extensive tests for possible\nunderstatement of","options":{"A":"Revenues","B":"Assets","C":"Capital","D":"Liabilities"},"correctAnswer":"D","topic":"Audit Principles & Evidence","standard":"Philippine Standards on Auditing (PSA)","rationale":"Under auditing standards and professional practice, Option D accurately reflects the applicable requirement. The auditor gathers sufficient appropriate evidence to provide reasonable assurance regarding the relevant management assertions and financial statement disclosures.","distractorNotes":"The other choices are either inconsistent with Philippine auditing standards or represent incorrect procedures for this engagement stage."},{"id":"AT-100-34","set":"AT-100","num":34,"question":"Which of the following procedures would on auditor most likely\nperform in planning a financial statement audit","options":{"A":"Inquiring of the client's legal counsel concerning pending litigation","B":"Examining computer generated exception reports to verify the\neffectiveness of internal control","C":"Searching for unauthorized transactions that may aid in detecting\nunrecorded liabilities","D":"Comparing the financial statements to anticipated results."},"correctAnswer":"D","topic":"Audit Principles & Evidence","standard":"Philippine Standards on Auditing (PSA)","rationale":"Under auditing standards and professional practice, Option D accurately reflects the applicable requirement. The auditor gathers sufficient appropriate evidence to provide reasonable assurance regarding the relevant management assertions and financial statement disclosures.","distractorNotes":"The other choices are either inconsistent with Philippine auditing standards or represent incorrect procedures for this engagement stage."},{"id":"AT-100-35","set":"AT-100","num":35,"question":"Which of the following characteristics would most likely heighten\nan auditors concern about the risk of material misstatements in an\nentity‘s financial statements","options":{"A":"the entity industry is experiencing declining customer demand","B":"employees who handle cash receipts are not bonded","C":"bank reconciliations usually include in- transit deposits","D":"equipment is often sold at a loss before being fully depreciated"},"correctAnswer":"A","topic":"Audit Principles & Evidence","standard":"Philippine Standards on Auditing (PSA)","rationale":"Under auditing standards and professional practice, Option A accurately reflects the applicable requirement. The auditor gathers sufficient appropriate evidence to provide reasonable assurance regarding the relevant management assertions and financial statement disclosures.","distractorNotes":"The other choices are either inconsistent with Philippine auditing standards or represent incorrect procedures for this engagement stage."},{"id":"AT-100-36","set":"AT-100","num":36,"question":"Management philosophy and operating style most likely would have a\nsignificant influence on an entity‘s control environment when","options":{"A":"the internal auditor reports directly to management","B":"management is dominated by one individual","C":"accurate management job descriptions delineate specific duties","D":"the audit committee actively oversees the financial reporting process"},"correctAnswer":"B","topic":"Internal Control & Control Risk","standard":"PSA 315 & PSA 330 (Internal Control & Tests of Controls)","rationale":"Under PSA 315, internal control is designed, implemented, and maintained to provide reasonable assurance regarding reliability of financial reporting, effectiveness and efficiency of operations, and compliance with applicable 