ditor gathers sufficient appropriate evidence to provide reasonable assurance regarding the relevant management assertions and financial statement disclosures.","distractorNotes":"The other choices are either inconsistent with Philippine auditing standards or represent incorrect procedures for this engagement stage."},{"id":"AT-100-39","set":"AT-100","num":39,"question":"Which of the following would most likely be an advantage in using\nclassical variables sampling rather than probability-proportional-to-\nsize (PPS) sampling","options":{"A":"An estimate of the standard deviation of the population's recorded\namounts is not required","B":"The auditor rarely needs the assistance of a computer program to\ndesign an efficient sample","C":"inclusion of zero and negative balances generally does not require\nspecial design considerations","D":"Any amount that is individually significant is automatically\nidentified and selected."},"correctAnswer":"C","topic":"Audit Sampling (Statistical & Non-Statistical)","standard":"PSA 530 - Audit Sampling","rationale":"PSA 530 defines audit sampling as the application of audit procedures to less than 100% of items within a population of audit relevance such that all sampling units have a chance of selection in order to provide the auditor with a reasonable basis on which to draw conclusions about the entire population.","distractorNotes":"Other options misstate the relationship between sampling risk parameters (alpha/beta risk, tolerable misstatement, and sample size)."},{"id":"AT-100-40","set":"AT-100","num":40,"question":"A third party sues a public accounting firm for negligence under\ncommon law on the basis of materially false financial statements. Which\nof the following is the firm's defense","options":{"A":"Lack of privity","B":"Lack of reliance","C":"Lack of intent","D":"Contributory negligence"},"correctAnswer":"A","topic":"Audit Principles & Evidence","standard":"Philippine Standards on Auditing (PSA)","rationale":"Under au