 procedure, has\nlimited usefulness. However, it is used in a number of different audit\nsituations. Which of the following statements is true regarding\nobservation as an audit technique","options":{"A":"It is the most effective audit methodology to use in filling out\ninternal control questionnaires","B":"It is the most persuasive technique to learn how transactions are\nreally processed during the period under audit","C":"It is rarely sufficient to satisfy any audit assertion other than\nexistence","D":"It is the most persuasive audit technique for determining if fraud\nhas really occurred"},"correctAnswer":"C","topic":"Audit Principles & Evidence","standard":"Philippine Standards on Auditing (PSA)","rationale":"Under auditing standards and professional practice, Option C accurately reflects the applicable requirement. The auditor gathers sufficient appropriate evidence to provide reasonable assurance regarding the relevant management assertions and financial statement disclosures.","distractorNotes":"The other choices are either inconsistent with Philippine auditing standards or represent incorrect procedures for this engagement stage."},{"id":"AT-100-71","set":"AT-100","num":71,"question":"Which of the following statements is not true? “The evidence\ngathering technique of inquiry","options":{"A":"Cannot be regarded as conclusive.”","B":"Requires the gathering of corroborative evidence.”","C":"Is the auditor‘s principal method of evaluating the client‘s internal\ncontrol structure.”","D":"Does not provide evidence from an independent source.”"},"correctAnswer":"C","topic":"Audit Principles & Evidence","standard":"Philippine Standards on Auditing (PSA)","rationale":"Under auditing standards and professional practice, Option C accurately reflects the applicable requirement. The auditor gathers sufficient appropriate evidence to provide reasonable assurance regarding the relevant management assertions and financial statement disclosures.","distractorNotes":"The other choices are either inconsistent with Philippine auditing standards or represent incorrect procedures for this engagement stage."},{"id":"AT-100-72","set":"AT-100","num":72,"question":"Which of the following statements concerning the sample size is true","options":{"A":"An increase in the tolerable occurrence rate, other factors remaining\nunchanged, increases the sample size","B":"The higher the expected occurrence rate, other factors remaining\nunchanged, the larger will be the sample size","C":"The more critical the attribute being tested, the higher will be the\ntolerable occurrence rate set by the auditor, and the larger will be the\nsample size","D":"The lower the acceptable risk of underassessment of control risk, the\nsmaller will be the sample size be."},"correctAnswer":"B","topic":"Audit Sampling (Statistical & Non-Statistical)","standard":"PSA 530 - Audit Sampling","rationale":"The tolerable rate of deviation (or failure rate) is the maximum rate set by the auditor for which control risk can still be supported. An auditor estimates this parameter during planning to determine the appropriate sample size necessary to achieve the desired confidence level.","distractorNotes":"Tolerable rate does not determine the sampling approach (statistical vs nonstatistical) nor does it define the population boundaries."},{"id":"AT-100-73","set":"AT-100","num":73,"question":"Which of the following statements regarding statistical sampling in\nauditing is true","options":{"A":"In as much as audits are test-based, generally accepted auditing\nstandards require the use of statistical sampling methods whenever the\nauditor decided to examine only a part of the population","B":"Although statistical sampling may be applied to test controls, it is\nrequired for substantive testing purposes","C":"Sampling methods are used by auditors in both testing of controls and\nsubstantive testing","D":"Statistical sampling methods are more appropriate for testing of\ncontrols when the auditor elects to reprocess transactions, than when\ncontrols are tested by means of document examination."},"correctAnswer":"C","topic":"Audit Sampling (Statistical & Non-Statistical)","standard":"PSA 530 - Audit Sampling","rationale":"PSA 530 defines audit sampling as the application of audit procedures to less than 100% of items within a population of audit relevance such that all sampling units have a chance of selection in order to provide the auditor with a reasonable basis on which to draw conclusions about the entire population.","distractorNotes":"Other options misstate the relationship between sampling risk parameters (alpha/beta risk, tolerable misstatement, and sample size)."},{"id":"AT-100-74","set":"AT-100","num":74,"question":"Which of the following statement is true","options":{"A":"The audit procedures will vary as a result of using either statistical\nor non-statistical sampling","B":"The audit procedures will be the same for either statistical or non-\nstatistical sampling, but they must be performed differently for each","C":"Statistical sampling requires quantitative audit procedures whereas\nnon-statistical sampling requires judgmental audit procedures","D":"The same audit procedures are performed in the same manner for either\nstatistical or non-statistical sampling."},"correctAnswer":"D","topic":"Audit Principles & Evidence","standard":"Philippine Standards on Auditing (PSA)","rationale":"Under auditing standards and professional practice, Option D accurately reflects the applicable requirement. The auditor gathers sufficient appropriate evidence to provide reasonable assurance regarding the relevant management assertions and financial statement disclosures.","distractorNotes":"The other choices are either inconsistent with Philippine auditing standards or represent incorrect procedures for this engagement stage."},{"id":"AT-100-75","set":"AT-100","num":75,"question":"Which of the following statements is true about non-statistical\nsampling, in tests of controls","options":{"A":"Non-statistical sampling plans provide a quantitative measure of\nsampling risk","B":"The auditor's judgment in non-statistical sampling is guided by\nclassical statistical sampling concepts","C":"The calculated non-statistical sample should never be altered by the\nauditor","D":"The auditor considers the same parameters when determining a non-\nstatistical sample size as when determining a statistical sample size"},"correctAnswer":"D","topic":"Audit Sampling (Statistical & Non-Statistical)","standard":"PSA 530 - Audit Sampling","rationale":"PSA 530 defines audit sampling as the application of audit procedures to less than 100% of items within a population of audit relevance such that all sampling units have a chance of selection in order to provide the auditor with a reasonable basis on which to draw conclusions about the entire population.","distractorNotes":"Other options misstate the relationship between sampling risk parameters (alpha/beta risk, tolerable misstatement, and sample size)."},{"id":"AT-100-76","set":"AT-100","num":76,"question":"An auditor is testing credit authorization procedures by examining\nsales invoices for credit approval by the credit department. The\nprocedures will be considered to be working adequately if 96% of all\nsales invoices either indicate approval or are cash sales. The auditor\nselects a random sample of 100 invoices. In this situation, which of the\nfollowing outcomes illustrates underassessment","options":{"A":"The auditor finds five deviations and concludes that procedures work\ninadequately. The actual population deviation rate is 2%","B":"The auditor finds no deviations and concludes that, procedures work\nadequately. The true population deviation rate is 5%","C":"The auditor finds no deviations and concludes that the procedures\nwork adequately. The true population deviation rate is 2%","D":"The auditor finds five deviations and concludes that procedures work\ninadequately. The true population deviation rate is 6%."},"correctAnswer":"B","topic":"Audit Sampling (Statistical & Non-Statistical)","standard":"PSA 530 - Audit Sampling","rationale":"Underassessment of control risk (the risk of assessing control risk too low) occurs when the sample result indicates that internal controls are effective when in fact the true population deviation rate exceeds the tolerable rate. This directly impairs audit effectiveness because the auditor inappropriately reduces substantive testing.","distractorNotes":"Assessing control risk too high (overassessment) causes audit inefficiency because the auditor performs more substantive work than necessary, but it does not reduce audit effectiveness."},{"id":"AT-100-77","set":"AT-100","num":77,"question":"At times a sample may indicate that the auditor's assessed level of\ncontrol risk for a given control is reasonable when, in fact, the true\n\n\n\n\ncompliance rate does not justify the assessed level. This situation\nillustrates the risk of","options":{"A":"assessing control risk too low","B":"incorrect precision","C":"assessing control risk too high","D":"incorrect rejection"},"correctAnswer":"A","topic":"Internal Control & Control Risk","standard":"PSA 315 & PSA 330 (Internal Control & Tests of Controls)","rationale":"Under PSA 315, internal control is designed, implemented, and maintained to provide reasonable assurance regarding reliability of financial reporting, effectiveness and efficiency of operations, and compliance with applicable laws and regulations.","distractorNotes":"Other options reflect management operational targets or clerical procedures that do not directly address internal control objectives."},{"id":"AT-100-78","set":"AT-100","num":78,"question":"As a result of tests of controls, an auditor under assessed control\nrisk and decreased substantive testing. This underassessment occurred\nbecause the true occurrence rate in the population was","options":{"A":"Less than the risk of underassessment in the auditor's sample","B":"Less than the occurrence rate in the auditor's sample","C":"More than the risk of underassessment in the auditor's sample","D":"More than the occurrence rate in the auditor‘s sample."},"correctAnswer":"D","topic":"Audit Sampling (Statistical & Non-Statistical)","standard":"PSA 530 - Audit Sampling","rationale":"Underassessment of control risk (the risk of assessing control risk too low) occurs when the sample result indicates that internal controls are effective when in fact the true population deviation rate exceeds the tolerable rate. This directly impairs audit effectiveness because the auditor inappropriately reduces substantive testing.","distractorNotes":"Assessing control risk too high (overassessment) causes audit inefficiency because the auditor performs more substantive work than necessary, but it does not reduce audit effectiveness."},{"id":"AT-100-79","set":"AT-100","num":79,"question":"For variables sampling purposes, changes in certain parameters affect\nsample size positively while changes in others have a negative effect.\nIn this regard, which of the following statements is true","options":{"A":"An increase in beta risk reduces sample size","B":"Population size affects sample size inversely, i.e., as population\nsize increases, sample size decreases","C":"An increase in alpha risk, increases sample size","D":"As materiality (M) increases, sample size increases."},"correctAnswer":"A","topic":"Audit Sampling (Statistical & Non-Statistical)","standard":"PSA 530 - Audit Sampling","rationale":"The tolerable rate of deviation (or failure rate) is the maximum rate set by the auditor for which control risk can still be supported. An auditor estimates this parameter during planning to determine the appropriate sample size necessary to achieve the desired confidence level.","distractorNotes":"Tolerable rate does not determine the sampling approach (statistical vs nonstatistical) nor does it define the population boundaries."},{"id":"AT-100-80","set":"AT-100","num":80,"question":"Although most substantive testing is performed during the final\naudit, some substantive tests may be done during the interim period.\nWhich of the following statements concerning the timing of substantive\ntests is true","options":{"A":"When internal control is weak, extensive substantive testing should\nbe performed during the interim audit","B":"Substantive testing should be performed during the interim audit only\nunder conditions of excellent internal control","C":"As a general rule, the auditor performs substantive tests of balances\nas of the balance sheet date and tests of transactions during the interim\nas well as the year-end audit","D":"If internal control is weak, the auditor should confirm accounts\nreceivable as of a point in time at least one month prior to the client's\nfiscal year-end."},"correctAnswer":"C","topic":"Substantive Testing & Confirmations","standard":"PSA 500, 501 & 505 (Audit Evidence & Confirmations)","rationale":"Under PSA 500 and 505, substantive procedures are designed to detect material misstatements at the assertion level. External confirmations provide highly reliable third-party evidence regarding balances and contract terms.","distractorNotes":"Other options address distinct assertions that require different procedures such as recalculation, observation, or inquiry."},{"id":"AT-100-81","set":"AT-100","num":81,"question":"Which of the following is true about the audit procedure of\nconfirming accounts payable","options":{"A":"Confirmation of payables is most appropriate when the auditor expects\nunderstatement errors","B":"It is not productive to mail second requests","C":"The auditor is not required by current professional pronouncements\nto justify his or her opinion on financial statements when payables are\nnot confirmed","D":"Payables are usually confirmed as of an interim date."},"correctAnswer":"C","topic":"Substantive Testing & Confirmations","standard":"PSA 500, 501 & 505 (Audit Evidence & Confirmations)","rationale":"In accounts payable, the auditor's primary objective is detecting unrecorded liabilities (understatement). Unlike accounts receivable where large positive balances are confirmed, payable confirmations emphasize major vendors regardless of the recorded balance, especially those with small or zero year-end balances.","distractorNotes":"Confirmations for payables are not required by standards if adequate alternative procedures (search for unrecorded liabilities, review of subsequent disbursements) are conducted."},{"id":"AT-100-82","set":"AT-100","num":82,"question":"Assume an auditor's interim consideration of internal control in the\nexpenditure/disbursement cycle reveals that control risk can be assessed\nbelow the maximum and detection risk above the minimum for some\nassertions. Based on the foregoing, which of the following is true about\nthe substantive tests applied to accounts payable","options":{"A":"The auditor is more apt to confirm payable balances","B":"The auditor is less apt to perform substantive tests at the balance\nsheet date","C":"The auditor is more apt to increase the extent of substantive tests","D":"The auditor is more apt to ignore the risk of incorrect acceptance\nwhen sampling accounts payable."},"correctAnswer":"B","topic":"Internal Control & Control Risk","standard":"PSA 315 & PSA 330 (Internal Control & Tests of Controls)","rationale":"Under PSA 315, internal control is designed, implemented, and maintained to provide reasonable assurance regarding reliability of financial reporting, effectiveness and efficiency of operations, and compliance with applicable laws and regulations.","distractorNotes":"Other options reflect management operational targets or clerical procedures that do not directly address internal control objectives."},{"id":"AT-100-83","set":"AT-100","num":83,"question":"Which of the following statements is true with respect to management\nrepresentations","options":{"A":"Management representations are dated as of the balance sheet date","B":"Management representations may serve as a substitute to various types\nof substantive procedures","C":"Management representations are signed by the auditor and delivered\nto the client's officers","D":"Management representations are used to corroborate information\nobtained during the audit."},"correctAnswer":"D","topic":"Audit Principles & Evidence","standard":"Philippine Standards on Auditing (PSA)","rationale":"Under auditing standards and professional practice, Option D accurately reflects the applicable requirement. The auditor gathers sufficient appropriate evidence to provide reasonable assurance regarding the relevant management assertions and financial statement disclosures.","distractorNotes":"The other choices are either inconsistent with Philippine auditing standards or represent incorrect procedures for this engagement stage."},{"id":"AT-100-84","set":"AT-100","num":84,"question":"Which of the following statements is true","options":{"A":"It is more difficult to discover unrecorded transactions or events\nthan to verify recorded information","B":"It is more difficult to verily recorded information than to discover\nunrecorded transactions or events","C":"It is equally difficult to verify recorded information and to discover\nunrecorded transactions or events","D":"None of the given choices is true."},"correctAnswer":"A","topic":"Audit Principles & Evidence","standard":"Philippine Standards on Auditing (PSA)","rationale":"Under auditing standards and professional practice, Option A accurately reflects the applicable requirement. The auditor gathers sufficient appropriate evidence to provide reasonable assurance regarding the relevant management assertions and financial statement disclosures.","distractorNotes":"The other choices are either inconsistent with Philippine auditing standards or represent incorrect procedures for this engagement stage."},{"id":"AT-100-85","set":"AT-100","num":85,"question":"Which of the following statements is true concerning analytical\nprocedures","options":{"A":"Analytical procedures usually involve comparisons of ratios developed\nfrom recorded amounts with assertions developed by the management","B":"Analytical procedures used in planning an audit ordinarily use data\naggregated at a high level","C":"Analytical procedures can replace tests of controls in gathering\nevidence to support the assessed level of control risk","D":"Analytical procedures are more efficient, but not more effective,\nthan tests of details and transactions"},"correctAnswer":"B","topic":"Audit Principles & Evidence","standard":"Philippine Standards on Auditing (PSA)","rationale":"Under auditing standards and professional practice, Option B accurately reflects the applicable requirement. The auditor gathers sufficient appropriate evidence to provide reasonable assurance regarding the relevant management assertions and financial statement disclosures.","distractorNotes":"The other choices are either inconsistent with Philippine auditing standards or represent incorrect procedures for this engagement stage."},{"id":"AT-100-86","set":"AT-100","num":86,"question":"Which of the following statements regarding assurance services is/are\ntrue with respect to internal auditors?\nI. Assurance services by internal auditors can be provided only to\ninternal management and the audit committee: that is. They cannot be\nprovided to outside entities.\nII. Assurance services require the specification of assertions to be\ntested. III. Assurance services can be provided bray in the areas of\ncontrols and financial reporting","options":{"A":"I and II","B":"II only","C":"I, II, and III","D":"None of the above"},"correctAnswer":"B","topic":"Audit Principles & Evidence","standard":"Philippine Standards on Auditing (PSA)","rationale":"Under auditing standards and professional practice, Option B accurately reflects the applicable requirement. The auditor gathers sufficient appropriate evidence to provide reasonable assurance regarding the relevant management assertions and financial statement disclosures.","distractorNotes":"The other choices are either inconsistent with Philippine auditing standards or represent incorrect procedures for this engagement stage."},{"id":"AT-100-87","set":"AT-100","num":87,"question":"Which of the following statements is/are true regarding independence\nand objectivity as applied to internal auditing?\nI. Independence is a departmental feature that affects the scope of\naudits.\nII. Only the audit committee can determine independence.\nIII. Objectivity is a personal feature that is to be exhibited by all\ninternal audit team members on an audit","options":{"A":"I and II","B":"I and III","C":"I, II, and III","D":"Ill only"},"correctAnswer":"B","topic":"Professional Ethics & Independence","standard":"Philippine Code of Ethics for Professional Accountants","rationale":"The Philippine Code of Ethics establishes five fundamental principles (Integrity, Objectivity, Professional Competence & Due Care, Confidentiality, and Professional Behavior) and a conceptual framework requiring CPAs to identify, evaluate, and address threats to compliance through appropriate safeguards.","distractorNotes":"Other choices either misclassify the threat category or propose actions that do not satisfy required ethical safeguards."},{"id":"AT-100-88","set":"AT-100","num":88,"question":"Which of the following statements is true concerning interim\nfinancial information","options":{"A":"An audit of interim financial information is required for SEC-\nregistered companies","B":"The accountant needs to obtain sufficient knowledge of the entity's\nbusiness and industry before undertaking an engagement on interim\nfinancial information","C":"An accountant may not report on financial information presented\nseparately from the audited financial statements","D":"Interim financial information may not be included as part of a note\nto audited financial statements."},"correctAnswer":"B","topic":"Audit Principles & Evidence","standard":"Philippine Standards on Auditing (PSA)","rationale":"Under auditing standards and professional practice, Option B accurately reflects the applicable requirement. The auditor gathers sufficient appropriate evidence to provide reasonable assurance regarding the relevant management assertions and financial statement disclosures.","distractorNotes":"The other choices are either inconsistent with Philippine auditing standards or represent incorrect procedures for this engagement stage."},{"id":"AT-100-89","set":"AT-100","num":89,"question":"Which of the following statement is true","options":{"A":"If the auditor assesses the level of control risk at the maximum, no\ndocumentation of the reason is necessary","B":"If the auditor assesses the level control risk at less than maximum,\nno documentation of the reason is necessary","C":"If the auditor assesses the level of control risk at the maximum,\ndocumentation of the reason is necessary","D":"All of the given choices"},"correctAnswer":"A","topic":"Audit Principles & Evidence","standard":"Philippine Standards on Auditing (PSA)","rationale":"Under auditing standards and professional practice, Option A accurately reflects the applicable requirement. The auditor gathers sufficient appropriate evidence to provide reasonable assurance regarding the relevant management assertions and financial statement disclosures.","distractorNotes":"The other choices are either inconsistent with Philippine auditing standards or represent incorrect procedures for this engagement stage."},{"id":"AT-100-90","set":"AT-100","num":90,"question":"Which of the following statements about the existence and\ncompleteness objectives are not true","options":{"A":"The existence and completeness objectives emphasize opposite audit\nconcerns","B":"Existence deals with overstatements and completeness deals with\nunderstatements","C":"Existence deals with understatements and completeness' deals with\noverstatements","D":"The completeness objective deals with unrecorded transactions"},"correctAnswer":"C","topic":"Audit Principles & Evidence","standard":"Philippine Standards on Auditing (PSA)","rationale":"Under auditing standards and professional practice, Option C accurately reflects the applicable requirement. The auditor gathers sufficient appropriate evidence to provide reasonable assurance regarding the relevant management assertions and financial statement disclosures.","distractorNotes":"The other choices are either inconsistent with Philippine auditing standards or represent incorrect procedures for this engagement stage."},{"id":"AT-100-91","set":"AT-100","num":91,"question":"Which of the following statements about the failure of the auditor\nof detecting material misstatements always true","options":{"A":"The auditor's failure to discover material misstatements is an\nevidence of inadequate planning: performance or judgment","B":"The auditor did not comply with Philippine Standards on Auditing","C":"The auditor has the burden of defending the quality of his audit","D":"Such a failure is an indication of the absence of the auditor‘s,\nprofessional competence and due care."},"correctAnswer":"C","topic":"Audit Principles & Evidence","standard":"Philippine Standards on Auditing (PSA)","rationale":"Under auditing standards and professional practice, Option C accurately reflects the applicable requirement. The auditor gathers sufficient appropriate evidence to provide reasonable assurance regarding the relevant management assertions and financial statement disclosures.","distractorNotes":"The other choices are either inconsistent with Philippine auditing standards or represent incorrect procedures for this engagement stage."},{"id":"AT-100-92","set":"AT-100","num":92,"question":"Which of the following statements concerning analytical procedures\nis true","options":{"A":"Analytical procedures may be omitted entirely for some financial\nstatement audits","B":"Analytical procedures used in planning the audit should not use\nnonfinancial information","C":"Analytical procedures usually are effective and efficient for tests\nof controls","D":"Analytical procedures alone may provide the appropriate level of\nassurance for some assertions"},"correctAnswer":"D","topic":"Analytical Procedures","standard":"PSA 520 (Analytical Procedures)","rationale":"Under PSA 520, analytical procedures involve evaluations of financial information made by an analysis of relationships among financial and non-financial data, identifying inconsistencies or unexpected fluctuations.","distractorNotes":"Other options do not align with the purpose, timing, or predictive nature of analytical procedures as defined under PSA 520."},{"id":"AT-100-93","set":"AT-100","num":93,"question":"Which of the following is true of the report based on-an agreed-upon\nprocedures engagement","options":{"A":"The report is restricted to those parties who have agreed to those\nspecific procedures to be performed","B":"The CPA provides the recipients of the report a tinged assurance\nreasonableness of the assertion(s) presented in the financial\ninformation","C":"The report states that the auditor has not recognized any basis that\nrequires revision of financial statements","D":"The report should state that the procedures performed are limited to\nanalytical procedures and inquiry."},"correctAnswer":"A","topic":"Agreed-Upon Procedures","standard":"PSRS 4400 - Engagements to Perform Agreed-Upon Procedures","rationale":"In an agreed-upon procedures engagement, the auditor provides a report of factual findings based on specific procedures agreed between the auditor and third parties. No assurance is expressed; instead, users evaluate the procedures and findings themselves. Distribution of the report is restricted to those specified parties who agreed to the procedures.","distractorNotes":"Agreed-upon procedures do not provide positive or negative assurance, and the report cannot be distributed for general public use."},{"id":"AT-100-94","set":"AT-100","num":94,"question":"Audit risk consists of inherent risk, control risk; and detection\nrisk. Which of the following statements is true","options":{"A":"Cash is more susceptible to theft than an inventory of coal because\nit has a greater inherent risk","B":"The risk that material misstatement will not be prevented or detected\non a timely basis by internal control can be reduced to zero by effective\ncontrols","C":"Detection risk is a function of the efficiency of an auditing\nprocedure","D":"The existing levels of inherent risk, control risk, and detection\nrisk can be changed at the discretion of the auditor."},"correctAnswer":"A","topic":"Internal Control & Control Risk","standard":"PSA 315 & PSA 330 (Internal Control & Tests of Controls)","rationale":"Under PSA 315, internal control is designed, implemented, and maintained to provide reasonable assurance regarding reliability of financial reporting, effectiveness and efficiency of operations, and compliance with applicable laws and regulations.","distractorNotes":"Other options reflect management operational targets or clerical procedures that do not directly address internal control objectives."},{"id":"AT-100-95","set":"AT-100","num":95,"question":"An internal auditor is testing purchase order to detect possible\ninstances of fraudulent activity by an employee. Believing the occurrence\nrate of the fraudulent purchase orders to be quite low (almost zero) the\ninternal auditor would like to specify the probability of observing at\nleast one irregularity if its true rate of occurrence is greater than\nexpected. The most appropriate sampling technique for this situation is","options":{"A":"Sequential sampling","B":"Variable estimation sampling","C":"Attribute estimation sampling","D":"Discovery sampling"},"correctAnswer":"D","topic":"Audit Sampling (Statistical & Non-Statistical)","standard":"PSA 530 - Audit Sampling","rationale":"PSA 530 defines audit sampling as the application of audit procedures to less than 100% of items within a population of audit relevance such that all sampling units have a chance of selection in order to provide the auditor with a reasonable basis on which to draw conclusions about the entire population.","distractorNotes":"Other options misstate the relationship between sampling risk parameters (alpha/beta risk, tolerable misstatement, and sample size)."},{"id":"AT-100-96","set":"AT-100","num":96,"question":"Which of the following is a true statement","options":{"A":"The extent of the audit procedures performed on the corresponding\nfigures is significantly less than scope of the audit for the current\nfigures","B":"When the financial statements of the prior period have been audited\nby another auditor, the current auditor must insist that there would be\ndivision of responsibility with respect to audit","C":"When the financial statements of the prior period have been audited\nby another auditor, the successor auditor must insist that audit of the\nfinancial statements of the prior periods be performed by the successor","D":"When the comparatives are presented as corresponding figures, the\nauditor must specifically refer to the predecessor in the introductory\nparagraph of the auditor's report."},"correctAnswer":"A","topic":"Audit Principles & Evidence","standard":"Philippine Standards on Auditing (PSA)","rationale":"Under auditing standards and professional practice, Option A accurately reflects the applicable requirement. The auditor gathers sufficient appropriate evidence to provide reasonable assurance regarding the relevant management assertions and financial statement disclosures.","distractorNotes":"The other choices are either inconsistent with Philippine auditing standards or represent incorrect procedures for this engagement stage."},{"id":"AT-100-97","set":"AT-100","num":97,"question":"Holding other planning considerations 