hich of the following is incorrect regarding engagement period","options":{"A":"The period of the engagement starts when the assurance team begins\nto perform assurance services and ends when the assurance report is\nissued, except when the assurance engagement is of a recurring nature","B":"If the assurance engagement is expected to recur, the period of the\nassurance engagement ends with the notification by either party that the\nprofessional relationship has terminated or the issuance of the final\nassurance report, whichever is earlier","C":"In the case of an audit engagement, the engagement period includes\nthe period covered by the financial statements reported on by the firm","D":"When an entity becomes an audit client during or after the period\ncovered by the financial statements that the firm will report on, the\nfirm should consider whether any threats to independence may be created\nby previous services provided to the audit client."},"correctAnswer":"B","topic":"Audit Principles & Evidence","standard":"Philippine Standards on Auditing (PSA)","rationale":"Under auditing standards and professional practice, Option B accurately reflects the applicable requirement. The auditor gathers sufficient appropriate evidence to provide reasonable assurance regarding the relevant management assertions and financial statement disclosures.","distractorNotes":"The other choices are either inconsistent with Philippine auditing standards or represent incorrect procedures for this engagement stage."},{"id":"AT-300-18","set":"AT-300","num":18,"question":"The following self-interest threat created would be so significant\nno safeguard could reduce the threat to an acceptable level, except","options":{"A":"If a firm, or a network firm, has a direct financial interest in an\naudit client of the firm","B":"If a fir, or a network firm, has a material indirect financial interest\nin an audit client of the firm","C":"If a firm, or a network firm, has a material financial interest in\nan entity that has a controlling interest in an audit client","D":"If the retirement benefit plan of a firm, or network firm, has a\nfinancial interest in an audit client"},"correctAnswer":"D","topic":"Professional Ethics & Independence","standard":"Philippine Code of Ethics for Professional Accountants","rationale":"The Philippine Code of Ethics establishes five fundamental principles (Integrity, Objectivity, Professional Competence & Due Care, Confidentiality, and Professional Behavior) and a conceptual framework requiring CPAs to identify, evaluate, and address threats to compliance through appropriate safeguards.","distractorNotes":"Other choices either misclassify the threat category or propose actions that do not satisfy required ethical safeguards."},{"id":"AT-300-19","set":"AT-300","num":19,"question":"The following loans and guarantees would not create a threat to\nindependence, except","options":{"A":"A loan from, or a guarantee thereof by, an assurance client that is\na bank or a similar institution, to the firm, provided the loan is made\nunder normal lending procedures, terms and requirements and the loan is\nimmaterial to both the firm and the assurance client","B":"A loan from, or a guarantee thereof by, an assurance client that is\na bank or a similar institution, to a member of the assurance team or\ntheir immediate family, provided the loan is made under normal lending\nprocedures, terms and requirements","C":"Deposits made 1* or brokerage accounts of, a firm or a member of the\nassurance team with an assurance client that is a bank, broker or similar\ninstitution, provided the deposit or account is held under normal\ncommercial terms","D":"If the firm, or a member of the assurance team, makes a loan to an\nassurance client that is not a bank or similar institution, or guarantees\nsuch an assurance clients‘ borrowings"},"correctAnswer":"D","topic":"Professional Ethics & Independence","standard":"Philippine Code of Ethics for Professional Accountants","rationale":"The Philippine Code of Ethics establishes five fundamental principles (Integrity, Objectivity, Professional Competence & Due Care, Confidentiality, and Professional Behavior) and a conceptual framework requiring CPAs to identify, evaluate, and address threats to compliance through appropriate safeguards.","distractorNotes":"Other choices either misclassify the threat category or propose actions that do not satisfy required ethical safeguards."},{"id":"AT-300-20","set":"AT-300","num":20,"question":"When a firm or a member of the assurance team and the audit client\nor one of its officers hold interest in a closely-held entity, a threat\nto independence is not created, except","options":{"A":"The relationship is clearly insignificant to the member of the\nassurance team and the audit client","B":"The relationship is other than insignificant acceptable for indirect\nfinancial interest","C":"The interest held is immaterial to the investors or group of\ninvestors","D":"The interest does not give the investor, or group the ability to\ncontrol the closely-held entity."},"correctAnswer":"B","topic":"Professional Ethics & Independence","standard":"Philippine Code of Ethics for Professional Accountants","rationale":"The Philippine Code of Ethics establishes five fundamental principles (Integrity, Objectivity, Professional Competence & Due Care, Confidentiality, and Professional Behavior) and a conceptual framework requiring CPAs to identify, evaluate, and address threats to compliance through appropriate safeguards.","distractorNotes":"Other choices either misclassify the threat category or propose actions that do not satisfy required ethical safeguards."},{"id":"AT-300-21","set":"AT-300","num":21,"question":"The following activities would generally create self-interest or\nself-review threats that are so significant and that only avoidance of\nthe activity or refusal to perform the assurance engagement would reduce\nthe threats to an acceptable level, except","options":{"A":"Authorizing, executing or consummating a transaction, or otherwise\nexercising authority on behalf of the assurance client, or having the\nauthority to do so","B":"Determining which recommendation of the firm should be implemented","C":"Reporting, in a management role, to those charged with governance","D":"Providing technical assistance and advice on accounting principles\nfor audit clients."},"correctAnswer":"D","topic":"Professional Ethics & Independence","standard":"Philippine Code of Ethics for Professional Accountants","rationale":"The Philippine Code of Ethics establishes five fundamental principles (Integrity, Objectivity, Professional Competence & Due Care, Confidentiality, and Professional Behavior) and a conceptual framework requiring CPAs to identify, evaluate, and address threats to compliance through appropriate safeguards.","distractorNotes":"Other choices either misclassify the threat category or propose actions that do not satisfy required ethical safeguards."},{"id":"AT-300-22","set":"AT-300","num":22,"question":"If firm, or network firm, personnel providing such assistance make\nmanagement decisions, the self-review threat created could not be reduced\nto an acceptable level by any safeguards. Examples of such managerial\ndecisions include the following, except","options":{"A":"Determining or changing journal entries, or the classifications for\naccounts or transactions or other accounting records without obtaining\nthe approval of the audit clients","B":"Authorizing or approving transactions","C":"Preparing source documents or originating data (including decisions\non evaluation assumptions), or making changes to such documents or data","D":"Assisting an audit client in resolving account reconciliation\nproblems."},"correctAnswer":"D","topic":"Professional Ethics & Independence","standard":"Philippine Code of Ethics for Professional Accountants","rationale":"The Philippine Code of Ethics establishes five fundamental principles (Integrity, Objectivity, Professional Competence & Due Care, Confidentiality, and Professional Behavior) and a conceptual framework requiring CPAs to identify, evaluate, and address threats to compliance through appropriate safeguards.","distractorNotes":"Other choices either misclassify the threat category or propose actions that do not satisfy required ethical safeguards."},{"id":"AT-300-23","set":"AT-300","num":23,"question":"The following services are considered to be a normal part of the\naudit process and do not, under circumstances, threaten independence,\nexcept","options":{"A":"Analyzing and accumulating information for regulatory reporting","B":"Assisting in the preparation of consolidated financial statements","C":"Drafting disclosures items","D":"Having custody of an assurance client's assets."},"correctAnswer":"D","topic":"Professional Ethics & Independence","standard":"Philippine Code of Ethics for Professional Accountants","rationale":"The Philippine Code of Ethics establishes five fundamental principles (Integrity, Objectivity, Professional Competence & Due Care, Confidentiality, and Professional Behavior) and a conceptual framework requiring CPAs to identify, evaluate, and address threats to compliance through appropriate safeguards.","distractorNotes":"Other choices either misclassify the threat category or propose actions that do not satisfy required ethical safeguards."},{"id":"AT-300-24","set":"AT-300","num":24,"question":"The safeguards necessary to reduce the threat created by providing\naccounting and bookkeeping services to an audit client that is not a\nlisted entity to an acceptable level might include the following, except","options":{"A":"Making arrangements so that such services are not performed by a\nmember of the assurance team","B":"Implementing policies and procedures to prohibit the individual\nproviding such services from making any managerial decisions on behalf\nof the audit client","C":"Requiring the source data for the accounting entries to be originated\nby the assurance team","D":"Obtaining audit client approval for any proposed journal entries or\nother changes affecting the financial statements."},"correctAnswer":"C","topic":"Professional Ethics & Independence","standard":"Philippine Code of Ethics for Professional Accountants","rationale":"The Philippine Code of Ethics establishes five fundamental principles (Integrity, Objectivity, Professional Competence & Due Care, Confidentiality, and Professional Behavior) and a conceptual framework requiring CPAs to identify, evaluate, and address threats to compliance through appropriate safeguards.","distractorNotes":"Other choices either misclassify the threat category or propose actions that do not satisfy required ethical safeguards."},{"id":"AT-300-25","set":"AT-300","num":25,"question":"The provision of accounting and bookkeeping services of a routine\nor mechanical nature to divisions or subsidiaries of listed audit clients\nwould not be seen as impairing independence with respect to the audit\nclient provided that the following conditions are met, except","options":{"A":"The services do not involve the exercise of judgment","B":"The divisions or subsidiaries for which the service is provided are\ncollectively immaterial to the audit client","C":"The services provided are collectively immaterial to the division or\nsubsidiary","D":"The fees to the firm, or network firm, from such services are\ncollectively significant."},"correctAnswer":"D","topic":"Professional Ethics & Independence","standard":"Philippine Code of Ethics for Professional Accountants","rationale":"The Philippine Code of Ethics establishes five fundamental principles (Integrity, Objectivity, Professional Competence & Due Care, Confidentiality, and Professional Behavior) and a conceptual framework requiring CPAs to identify, evaluate, and address threats to compliance through appropriate safeguards.","distractorNotes":"Other choices either misclassify the threat category or propose actions that do not satisfy required ethical safeguards."},{"id":"AT-300-26","set":"AT-300","num":26,"question":"The following would generally create a significant threat to\nindependence, except","options":{"A":"When a firm, or a network firm, performs a valuation service for an\naudit client for the purpose of making a filing or return to a tax\nauthority","B":"The firm provides formal taxation opinions and assistance in the\nresolution of tax disputes to an audit client","C":"The firm renders internal services involving an extension of the\nprocedures required to conduct an audit in accordance with Philippine\nStandards on Auditing to an audit client","D":"When a firm, or a network firm, provides assistance in the performance\nof a client's internal audit activities or undertakes the outsourcing\nof some of the activities."},"correctAnswer":"D","topic":"Professional Ethics & Independence","standard":"Philippine Code of Ethics for Professional Accountants","rationale":"The Philippine Code of Ethics establishes five fundamental principles (Integrity, Objectivity, Professional Competence & Due Care, Confidentiality, and Professional Behavior) and a conceptual framework requiring CPAs to identify, evaluate, and address threats to compliance through appropriate safeguards.","distractorNotes":"Other choices either misclassify the threat category or propose actions that do not satisfy required ethical safeguards."},{"id":"AT-300-27","set":"AT-300","num":27,"question":"The lending of staff by a firm to a financial statement audit client\nmay be made only on the understanding that the firm's personnel will not\nbe involved in the following, except","options":{"A":"Assembling the annual financial statements based recorded\ntransactions","B":"Making management decisions","C":"Approving or signing agreements or other similar documents","D":"Exercising discretionary authority to commit the client."},"correctAnswer":"A","topic":"Audit Principles & Evidence","standard":"Philippine Standards on Auditing (PSA)","rationale":"Under auditing standards and professional practice, Option A accurately reflects the applicable requirement. The auditor gathers sufficient appropriate evidence to provide reasonable assurance regarding the relevant management assertions and financial statement disclosures.","distractorNotes":"The other choices are either inconsistent with Philippine auditing standards or represent incorrect procedures for this engagement stage."},{"id":"AT-300-28","set":"AT-300","num":28,"question":"When independence is threatened by litigation between the member of\nthe assurance team and the client management, the following safeguards\nthat can reduce the effect to an acceptable level may be applied, except","options":{"A":"Involve an additional professional accountant in the firm who is not\na member of the assurance team to review the work done","B":"Disclose to the audit committee, or others charged with governance,\nthe extent and the nature of the litigation","C":"Remove the particular member of the assurance team who is involved\nin litigation hum cite engagement","D":"Submit a new engagement letter."},"correctAnswer":"D","topic":"Professional Ethics & Independence","standard":"Philippine Code of Ethics for Professional Accountants","rationale":"The Philippine Code of Ethics establishes five fundamental principles (Integrity, Objectivity, Professional Competence & Due Care, Confidentiality, and Professional Behavior) and a conceptual framework requiring CPAs to identify, evaluate, and address threats to compliance through appropriate safeguards.","distractorNotes":"Other choices either misclassify the threat category or propose actions that do not satisfy required ethical safeguards."},{"id":"AT-300-29","set":"AT-300","num":29,"question":"The objectives of the Philippine Accountancy Act of 2004 are the\nfollowing except","options":{"A":"The standardization and regulation of accounting education","B":"Examination for registration of certified public accountants","C":"Supervision, control, and regulation of the practice of accountancy","D":"Integration of accountancy profession."},"correctAnswer":"D","topic":"Regulation of the Profession (RA 9298)","standard":"Republic Act No. 9298 (Philippine Accountancy Act of 2004)","rationale":"The stated objectives of RA 9298 are: (a) the standardization and regulation of accounting education, (b) the examination for registration of certified public accountants, and (c) the supervision, control, and regulation of the practice of accountancy in the Philippines. Integration of the profession is a mandate of the APO (PICPA), not a statutory objective of the Act.","distractorNotes":"Standardization of education, CPA licensure examinations, and supervision/regulation of practice are explicitly codified in Section 3 of RA 9298."},{"id":"AT-300-30","set":"AT-300","num":30,"question":"The following is deemed a practice of accountancy, except","options":{"A":"Appointment to a position in the government that requires a CPA\nlicense as a prerequisite","B":"Employment as budget officer in a local government unit regardless\nof the officer being a holder of a CPA license or not","C":"Teaching professional subjects in a collegiate program leading to the\ndegree of Bachelor of Science in Accountancy","D":"Representing his clients before government agencies on tax and other\nmatters related to accounting."},"correctAnswer":"B","topic":"Audit Principles & Evidence","standard":"Philippine Standards on Auditing (PSA)","rationale":"Under auditing standards and professional practice, Option B accurately reflects the applicable requirement. The auditor gathers sufficient appropriate evidence to provide reasonable assurance regarding the relevant management assertions and financial statement disclosures.","distractorNotes":"The other choices are either inconsistent with Philippine auditing standards or represent incorrect procedures for this engagement stage."},{"id":"AT-300-31","set":"AT-300","num":31,"question":"The following are represented both to the Financial Reporting\nStandards Council (FRSC) and Auditing and Assurance Standards Council\n(AASC), except","options":{"A":"Bangko Sentral ng Pilipinas","B":"Securities and Exchange Commission","C":"Bureau of Internal Revenue","D":"Board of Accountancy"},"correctAnswer":"C","topic":"Regulation of the Profession (RA 9298)","standard":"Republic Act No. 9298 (Philippine Accountancy Act of 2004)","rationale":"RA 9298 regulates the practice of accountancy in the Philippines, governing the Board of Accountancy (BOA), the Professional Regulation Commission (PRC), standard-setting bodies (FRSC & AASC), and licensing qualifications.","distractorNotes":"Other options conflict with specific statutory provisions and regulatory frameworks outlined in the IRR of RA 9298."},{"id":"AT-300-32","set":"AT-300","num":32,"question":"All of the following are represented to the Financial Reporting\nStandards Council, except","options":{"A":"Commission on Higher Education","B":"Board of Accountancy","C":"Securities and Exchange Commission","D":"Bureau of Internal Revenue"},"correctAnswer":"A","topic":"Audit Principles & Evidence","standard":"Philippine Standards on Auditing (PSA)","rationale":"Under auditing standards and professional practice, Option A accurately reflects the applicable requirement. The auditor gathers sufficient appropriate evidence to provide reasonable assurance regarding the relevant management assertions and financial statement disclosures.","distractorNotes":"The other choices are either inconsistent with Philippine auditing standards or represent incorrect procedures for this engagement stage."},{"id":"AT-300-33","set":"AT-300","num":33,"question":"The Philippine Accountancy Act of 2004 provides that all working\npapers made during an audit shall be the property of the auditor. These\nworking papers shall include the following, except","options":{"A":"Working papers prepared by the CPA and his staff","B":"Analysis and schedule prepared and submitted to the auditor by his\nclient's staff","C":"Excerpts or copies of documents furnished to the auditor","D":"Any report submitted by the auditor to his client."},"correctAnswer":"D","topic":"Regulation of the Profession (RA 9298)","standard":"Republic Act No. 9298 (Philippine Accountancy Act of 2004)","rationale":"Section 29 of RA 9298 provides that all working papers, schedules, and memoranda made by a CPA during an audit remain the property of the CPA, EXCEPT for the final reports submitted by the auditor to the client.","distractorNotes":"Audit schedules prepared by client staff, copies of documents furnished to the auditor, and working papers prepared by audit staff all form part of the auditor's confidential workpapers."},{"id":"AT-300-34","set":"AT-300","num":34,"question":"A professional accountant has a professional duty or right to\ndisclose confidential information in each of the following, except","options":{"A":"To comply with technical standards and ethics requirements","B":"To disclose to the Bureau of Internal Revenue any fraudulent scheme\ncommitted by the client on payment of income tax","C":"To comply with the quality review of a member body or professional\nbody","D":"To respond to an inquiry or investigation by a member body or\nregulatory body."},"correctAnswer":"B","topic":"Audit Principles & Evidence","standard":"Philippine Standards on Auditing (PSA)","rationale":"Under auditing standards and professional practice, Option B accurately reflects the applicable requirement. The auditor gathers sufficient appropriate evidence to provide reasonable assurance regarding the relevant management assertions and financial statement disclosures.","distractorNotes":"The other choices are either inconsistent with Philippine auditing standards or represent incorrect procedures for this engagement stage."},{"id":"AT-300-35","set":"AT-300","num":35,"question":"The CPA must not subordinate his or her professional judgment to\nthat of others in every","options":{"A":"engagement","B":"audit engagement","C":"engagement except tax services","D":"engagement except management advisory services."},"correctAnswer":"A","topic":"Audit Principles & Evidence","standard":"Philippine Standards on Auditing (PSA)","rationale":"Under auditing standards and professional practice, Option A accurately reflects the applicable requirement. The auditor gathers sufficient appropriate evidence to provide reasonable assurance regarding the relevant management assertions and financial statement disclosures.","distractorNotes":"The other choices are either inconsistent with Philippine auditing standards or represent incorrect procedures for this engagement stage."},{"id":"AT-300-36","set":"AT-300","num":36,"question":"A CPA firm issues an unqualified opinion on financial statements\nthat were not prepared in accordance with GAAP. The CPA firm would have\nacted with fraud or its equivalent in all the following circumstances\nexcept where the firm","options":{"A":"intentionally disregards the truth","B":"has actual knowledge of fraud","C":"negligently performs auditing procedures","D":"intends to gain monetarily by concealing the fraud."},"correctAnswer":"C","topic":"Audit Opinions & Reports","standard":"PSA 700 / 705 / 706 (Auditor's Reports & Modifications)","rationale":"Under PSA 700 and PSA 705, the auditor evaluates whether the financial statements are prepared, in all material respects, in accordance with the applicable financial reporting framework, determining whether an unmodified, qualified, adverse, or disclaimer of opinion is required.","distractorNotes":"The other choices confuse the circumstances of GAAP departure (qualified/adverse) with scope restrictions (qualified/disclaimer) or emphasis paragraphs."},{"id":"AT-300-37","set":"AT-300","num":37,"question":"The auditor‘s report may be addressed to any of the following, except\nthe client‘s","options":{"A":"Stockholders","B":"Chief executive officer","C":"Board of directors","D":"Partners"},"correctAnswer":"B","topic":"Audit Principles & Evidence","standard":"Philippine Standards on Auditing (PSA)","rationale":"Under auditing standards and professional practice, Option B accurately reflects the applicable requirement. The auditor gathers sufficient appropriate evidence to provide reasonable assurance regarding the relevant management assertions and financial statement disclosures.","distractorNotes":"The other choices are either inconsistent with Philippine auditing standards or represent incorrect procedures for this engagement stage."},{"id":"AT-300-38","set":"AT-300","num":38,"question":"The standard audit report explains that a financial audit includes\nall of the following except","options":{"A":"Examining support for the amounts and disclosure in the financial\nstatements","B":"Assessing the level of control risk","C":"Assessing the accounting principles used and significant estimates\nmade by the management","D":"Evaluating the overall financial statement presentation"},"correctAnswer":"B","topic":"Audit Opinions & Reports","standard":"PSA 700 / 705 / 706 (Auditor's Reports & Modifications)","rationale":"Under PSA 700 and PSA 705, the auditor evaluates whether the financial statements are prepared, in all material respects, in accordance with the applicable financial reporting framework, determining whether an unmodified, qualified, adverse, or disclaimer of opinion is required.","distractorNotes":"The other choices confuse the circumstances of GAAP departure (qualified/adverse) with scope restrictions (qualified/disclaimer) or emphasis paragraphs."},{"id":"AT-300-39","set":"AT-300","num":39,"question":"Which of the following is ordinarily true of a modification of the\naudit report by adding an emphasis of matter paragraph","options":{"A":"The modification by adding an emphasis of matter paragraph is an\n\"except for\" qualification of opinion","B":"The emphasis of matter paragraph is a \"subject to qualification of\nopinion","C":"The emphasis of matter paragraph would ordinarily refer to the fact\nthat the auditor's opinion is not qualified","D":"The emphasis of matter paragraph is presented before the opinion\nparagraph"},"correctAnswer":"C","topic":"Audit Opinions & Reports","standard":"PSA 700 / 705 / 706 (Auditor's Reports & Modifications)","rationale":"Under PSA 706, an Emphasis of Matter paragraph draws attention to a matter appropriately presented or disclosed in the financial statements that is fundamental to users' understanding. The auditor explicitly states that the opinion is NOT qualified in respect of the matter emphasized.","distractorNotes":"An emphasis of matter is not an 'except for' qualification, does not express a 'subject to' opinion (which is prohibited under auditing standards), and is placed immediately after the opinion paragraph."},{"id":"AT-300-40","set":"AT-300","num":40,"question":"An explanatory paragraph may be added to the audit report while at\nthe same time issuing an unqualified opinion in all cases except when","options":{"A":"the client has changed an accounting principle with the agreement of\nthe auditor","B":"there is an immaterial departure from GAAP to ensure fair presentation\nwith the agreement of the auditor","C":"the audit opinion is partly based on the work of another auditor","D":"the audit work has been significantly limited by management."},"correctAnswer":"D","topic":"Audit Opinions & Reports","standard":"PSA 700 / 705 / 706 (Auditor's Reports & Modifications)","rationale":"Under PSA 700 and PSA 705, the auditor evaluates whether the financial statements are prepared, in all material respects, in accordance with the applicable financial reporting framework, determining whether an unmodified, qualified, adverse, or disclaimer of opinion is required.","distractorNotes":"The other choices confuse the circumstances of GAAP departure (qualified/adverse) with scope restrictions (qualified/disclaimer) or emphasis paragraphs."},{"id":"AT-300-41","set":"AT-300","num":41,"question":"An explanatory paragraph or modified wordings may be added to the\naudit report while at the same time issuing an unqualified opinion in\nall cases except when","options":{"A":"the client has changed an accounting principle with the agreement of\nthe auditor","B":"there is an immaterial departure from PFRS to ensure fair presentation\nwith the agreement of the auditor","C":"the audit opinion is partly based on the work of another auditor","D":"the audit work has been materially limited by management."},"correctAnswer":"D","topic":"Audit Opinions & Reports","standard":"PSA 700 / 705 / 706 (Auditor's Reports & Modifications)","rationale":"Under PSA 700 and PSA 705, the auditor evaluates whether the financial statements are prepared, in all material respects, in accordance with the applicable financial reporting framework, determining whether an unmodified, qualified, adverse, or disclaimer of opinion is required.","distractorNotes":"The other choices confuse the circumstances of GAAP departure (qualified/adverse) with scope restrictions (qualified/disclaimer) or emphasis paragraphs."},{"id":"AT-300-42","set":"AT-300","num":42,"question":"When there is a limitation in the scope of the audit that results\nto a disclaimer of opinion, the following paragraphs are modified,\nexcept","options":{"A":"Introductory paragraph","B":"Management's responsibility for the financial statements","C":"Auditor's responsibility","D":"Auditor's opinion."},"correctAnswer":"B","topic":"Audit Opinions & Reports","standard":"PSA 700 / 705 / 706 (Auditor's Reports & Modifications)","rationale":"Under PSA 705, a disclaimer of opinion is issued when the auditor is unable to obtain sufficient appropriate audit evidence (severe scope limitation) and concludes that the possible undetected misstatements could be both material and pervasive, or in extremely rare situations involving multiple pervasive uncertainties.","distractorNotes":"Material departures from GAAP/PFRS result in qualified or adverse opinions, not disclaimers."},{"id":"AT-300-43","set":"AT-300","num":43,"question":"If an amendment is necessary in the other information and the entity\nrefuses to make the amendment, the auditor, depending on particular\ncircumstance, may do any of the following, except","options":{"A":"Describe the material inconsistency as an emphasis of matter in a\nparagraph following the opinion paragraph","B":"The auditor may not issue the auditor's report","C":"The auditor may withdraw from the engagement","D":"The auditor to issue either a qualified or adverse opinion."},"correctAnswer":"D","topic":"Audit Principles & Evidence","standard":"Philippine Standards on Auditing (PSA)","rationale":"Under auditing standards and professional practice, Option D accurately reflects the applicable requirement. The auditor gathers sufficient appropriate evidence to provide reasonable assurance regarding the relevant management assertions and financial statement disclosures.","distractorNotes":"The other choices are either inconsistent with Philippine auditing standards or represent incorrect procedures for this engagement stage."},{"id":"AT-300-44","set":"AT-300","num":44,"question":"If a principal auditor decides to assume responsibility for another\nauditor's work, the principal auditor should consider performing all of\nthe following procedures except","options":{"A":"Performing a peer review of the other auditor","B":"Reviewing the audit program of the other auditor","C":"Reviewing the working papers of the other auditor","D":"Discussing the audit procedures and the results of the audit with\nother auditor."},"correctAnswer":"A","topic":"Audit Principles & Evidence","standard":"Philippine Standards on Auditing (PSA)","rationale":"Under auditing standards and professional practice, Option A accurately reflects the applicable requirement. The auditor gathers sufficient appropriate evidence to provide reasonable assurance regarding the relevant management assertions and financial statement disclosures.","distractorNotes":"The other choices are either inconsistent with Philippine auditing standards or represent incorrect procedures for this engagement stage."},{"id":"AT-300-45","set":"AT-300","num":45,"question":".Management‘s integrity affects all of the following risks except","options":{"A":"Enterprise risk","B":"Financial reporting risk","C":"Engagement risk","D":"All of the above risks are affected"},"correctAnswer":"D","topic":"Audit Principles & Evidence","standard":"Philippine Standards on Auditing (PSA)","rationale":"Under auditing standards and professional practice, Option D accurately reflects the applicable requirement. The auditor gathers sufficient appropriate evidence to provide reasonable assurance regarding the relevant management assertions and financial statement disclosures.","distractorNotes":"The other choices are either inconsistent with Philippine auditing standards or represent incorrect procedures for this engagement stage."},{"id":"AT-300-46","set":"AT-300","num":46,"question":"To obtain an understanding of the relevant policies and procedures\nof internal control, the auditor performs all of the following except","options":{"A":"Make inquiries","B":"Make observations","C":"Inspect documents and records","D":"Design substantive tests"},"correctAnswer":"D","topic":"Internal Control & Control Risk","standard":"PSA 315 & PSA 330 (Internal Control & Tests of Controls)","rationale":"Under PSA 315, internal control is designed, implemented, and maintained to provide reasonable assurance regarding reliability of financial reporting, effectiveness and efficiency of operations, and compliance with applicable laws and regulations.","distractorNotes":"Other options reflect management operational targets or clerical procedures that do not directly address internal control objectives."},{"id":"AT-300-47","set":"AT-300","num":47,"question":"The understanding of internal control that relates to a financial\nstatement assertion should be used to do all of the following except","options":{"A":"Determine inherent risk for that assertion","B":"identify types of potential misstatements for that assertion","C":"Consider factors that affect the risk of material misstatement for\nthat assertion and assess control risk","D":"Design substantive tests that correspond with the assessment of\ncontrol risk."},"correctAnswer":"A","topic":"Internal Control & Control Risk","standard":"PSA 315 & PSA 330 (Internal Control & Tests of Controls)","rationale":"Under PSA 315, internal control is designed, implemented, and maintained to provide reasonable assurance regarding reliability of financial reporting, effectiveness and efficiency of operations, and compliance with applicable laws and regulations.","distractorNotes":"Other options reflect management operational targets or clerical procedures that do not directly address internal control objectives."},{"id":"AT-300-48","set":"AT-300","num":48,"question":"Tests of controls are concerned primarily with each of the following\nquestions except","options":{"A":"How were the controls applied","B":"Why were the controls applied","C":"Were the necessary controls consistently performed","D":"By whom were the controls applied?"},"correctAnswer":"B","topic":"Internal Control & Control Risk","standard":"PSA 315 & PSA 330 (Internal Control & Tests of Controls)","rationale":"Under PSA 315, internal control is designed, implemented, and maintained to provide reasonable assurance regarding reliability of financial reporting, effectiveness and efficiency of operations, and compliance with applicable laws and regulations.","distractorNotes":"Other options reflect management operational targets or clerical procedures that do not directly address internal control objectives."},{"id":"AT-300-49","set":"AT-300","num":49,"question":"In considering the evidence needed to assess control risk during the\nperiod from interim to year-end, all of the following should be\nconsidered except the","options":{"A":"Significance of the assertion being tested","B":"Specific internal control policies and procedures tested during the\ninterim period","C":"Degree to which the policies and procedures were tested and the test\nresults","D":"Control risk on other assertions."},"correctAnswer":"D","topic":"Internal Control & Control Risk","standard":"PSA 315 & PSA 330 (Internal Control & Tests of Controls)","rationale":"Under PSA 315, internal control is designed, implemented, and maintained to provide reasonable assurance regarding reliability of financial reporting, effectiveness and efficiency of operations, and compliance with applicable laws and regulations.","distractorNotes":"Other options reflect management operational targets or clerical procedures that do not directly address internal control objectives."},{"id":"AT-300-50","set":"AT-300","num":50,"question":"The assessment of control risk can be made at any of the following\ntimes except","options":{"A":"Immediately after obtaining an understanding of internal control","B":"After some tests of controls are performed concurrently with obtaining\nan understanding","C":"After the performance of additional tests of controls designed to\nfurther lower the assessment of control risk","D":"After performing all the necessary substantive tests."},"correctAnswer":"D","topic":"Internal Control & Control Risk","standard":"PSA 315 & PSA 330 (Internal Control & Tests of Controls)","rationale":"Under PSA 315, internal control is designed, implemented, and maintained to provide reasonable assurance regarding reliability of financial reporting, effectiveness and efficiency of operations, and compliance with applicable laws and regulations.","distractorNotes":"Other options reflect management operational targets or clerical procedures that do not directly address internal control objectives."},{"id":"AT-300-51","set":"AT-300","num":51,"question":"To be competent, evidence must be all of the following except","options":{"A":"Sufficient","B":"Reliable","C":"Relevant","D":"Unbiased"},"correctAnswer":"A","topic":"Audit Principles & Evidence","standard":"Philippine Standards on Auditing (PSA)","rationale":"Under auditing standards and professional practice, Option A accurately reflects the applicable requirement. The auditor gathers sufficient appropriate evidence to provide reasonable assurance regarding the relevant management assertions and financial statement disclosures.","distractorNotes":"The other choices are either inconsistent with Philippine auditing standards or represent incorrect procedures for this engagement stage."},{"id":"AT-300-52","set":"AT-300","num":52,"question":"Although the validity of evidential matter is dependent on the\ncircumstances under which it is obtained, there are three general\npresumptions that have some usefulness. The situations given below\nindicate the relative reliability that a CPA has placed on two types of\nevidence obtained in different situations. Which of these is an exception\nto one of the general presumptions","options":{"A":"The CPA places more reliance on the balance in the scrap sales account\nat Plant A, where the CPA has made limited tests of transactions because\nof effective controls, than at Plant B where the CPA has made extensive\ntests of transactions because of ineffective controls","B":"The CPA places more reliance on the CPAs computation of interest\npayable on outstanding bonds than on the amount confirmed by the trustee","C":"The CPA places more reliance on the report of an expert on an inventory\nof precious gems than on the CPAs physical observation of the gems","D":"The CPA places more reliance on a schedule of insurance coverage\nobtained from the company‘s insurance agent than on one prepared by the\ninternal audit staff."},"correctAnswer":"C","topic":"Audit Principles & Evidence","standard":"Philippine Standards on Auditing (PSA)","rationale":"Under auditing standards and professional practice, Option C accurately reflects the applicable requirement. The auditor gathers sufficient appropriate evidence to provide reasonable assurance regarding the relevant management assertions and financial statement disclosures.","distractorNotes":"The other choices are either inconsistent with Philippine auditing standards or represent incorrect procedures for this engagement stage."},{"id":"AT-300-53","set":"AT-300","num":53,"question":"Each of the following might, by itself, form a valid basis for an\nauditor of deciding to omit a test except for the","options":{"A":"Difficulty and expense involved in testing a particular item","B":"Assessment of control risk at a low level","C":"Inherent risk involved","D":"Relationship between the cost obtaining evidence and its usefulness"},"correctAnswer":"A","topic":"Audit Principles & Evidence","standard":"Philippine Standards on Auditing (PSA)","rationale":"Under auditing standards and professional practice, Option A accurately reflects the applicable requirement. The auditor gathers sufficient appropriate evidence to provide reasonable assurance regarding the relevant management assertions and financial statement disclosures.","distractorNotes":"The other choices are either inconsistent with Philippine auditing standards or represent incorrect procedures for this engagement stage."},{"id":"AT-300-54","set":"AT-300","num":54,"question":"Financial statement assertions include all of the following except","options":{"A":"Occurrence","B":"Presentation and disclosure","C":"Consistency and comparability","D":"Completeness"},"correctAnswer":"C","topic":"Audit Principles & Evidence","standard":"Philippine Standards on Auditing (PSA)","rationale":"Under auditing standards and professional practice, Option C accurately reflects the applicable requirement. The auditor gathers sufficient appropriate evidence to provide reasonable assurance regarding the relevant management assertions and financial statement disclosures.","distractorNotes":"The other choices are either inconsistent with Philippine auditing standards or represent incorrect procedures for this engagement stage."},{"id":"AT-300-55","set":"AT-300","num":55,"question":"In using the work of a specialist, an understanding should exist\namong the auditor, the client, and the specialist as to the nature of\nthe work to be performed by the specialist. Preferably, the understanding\nshould be documented and would include all of the following except","options":{"A":"The objectives and scope of the specialist‘s work","B":"The specialist‘s representations as to his relationship, if any, to\nthe client","C":"The specialist‘s understanding of the auditor‘s corroborative use of\nthe specialist‘s findings in relation to the representations in the\nfinancial statements","D":"A statement that the methods or assumptions to be used are not\ninconsistent with those used by the client"},"correctAnswer":"D","topic":"Audit Principles & Evidence","standard":"Philippine Standards on Auditing (PSA)","rationale":"Under auditing standards and professional practice, Option D accurately reflects the applicable requirement. The auditor gathers sufficient appropriate evidence to provide reasonable assurance regarding the relevant management assertions and financial statement disclosures.","distractorNotes":"The other choices are either inconsistent with Philippine auditing standards or represent incorrect procedures for this engagement stage."},{"id":"AT-300-56","set":"AT-300","num":56,"question":"Determining whether amounts are in conformity with GAAP addresses\nthe proper measurement of assets, liabilities, revenues, and expenses\nwhich includes all of the following except","options":{"A":"The reasonableness of management‘s accounting estimates","B":"Proper application of valuation principles","C":"Proper application of matching principle","D":"The reasonableness of management‘s accounting policies"},"correctAnswer":"D","topic":"Audit Principles & Evidence","standard":"Philippine Standards on Auditing (PSA)","rationale":"Under auditing standards and professional practice, Option D accurately reflects the applicable requirement. The auditor gathers sufficient appropriate evidence to provide reasonable assurance regarding the relevant management assertions and financial statement disclosures.","distractorNotes":"The other choices are either inconsistent with Philippine auditing standards or represent incorrect procedures for this engagement stage."},{"id":"AT-300-57","set":"AT-300","num":57,"question":"Each audit program should have a column for all of the following\nexcept","options":{"A":"Audit procedures to be performed","B":"The initials of the auditor who performs each procedure","C":"The date that the performance of the procedure is performed and\ncompleted","D":"The test of controls related to each procedure"},"correctAnswer":"D","topic":"Audit Principles & Evidence","standard":"Philippine Standards on Auditing (PSA)","rationale":"Under auditing standards and professional practice, Option D accurately reflects the applicable requirement. The auditor gathers sufficient appropriate evidence to provide reasonable assurance regarding the relevant management assertions and financial statement disclosures.","distractorNotes":"The other choices are either inconsistent with Philippine auditing standards or represent incorrect procedures for this engagement stage."},{"id":"AT-300-58","set":"AT-300","num":58,"question":"When evaluating the planned level of substantive tests for each\nsignificant assertion, the auditor will consider the evidence obtained\nfrom all of the following except","options":{"A":"Procedures to understand the business and industry and related\nanalytical procedures that have been completed","B":"Evidence about the effectiveness of internal controls gained while\nobtaining an understanding of internal control structure","C":"The assessment of detection risk","D":"Evidence of effectiveness of computer control procedures and related\nfollow-up."},"correctAnswer":"C","topic":"Substantive Testing & Confirmations","standard":"PSA 500, 501 & 505 (Audit Evidence & Confirmations)","rationale":"Under PSA 500 and 505, substantive procedures are designed to detect material misstatements at the assertion level. External confirmations provide highly reliable third-party evidence regarding balances and contract terms.","distractorNotes":"Other options address distinct assertions that require different procedures such as recalculation, observation, or inquiry."},{"id":"AT-300-59","set":"AT-300","num":59,"question":"Choices about audit evidence are influenced by all of the following\nexcept","options":{"A":"The auditor‘s understanding of the business and industry","B":"Assessment of inherent and control risk","C":"Comparisons of the auditor‘s expectation of the financial statements\nwith the client‘s books and records","D":"Decisions about immaterial risk factors"},"correctAnswer":"D","topic":"Audit Principles & Evidence","standard":"Philippine Standards on Auditing (PSA)","rationale":"Under auditing standards and professional practice, Option D accurately reflects the applicable requirement. The auditor gathers sufficient appropriate evidence to provide reasonable assurance regarding the relevant management assertions and financial statement disclosures.","distractorNotes":"The other choices are either inconsistent with Philippine auditing standards or represent incorrect procedures for this engagement stage."},{"id":"AT-300-60","set":"AT-300","num":60,"question":"If the auditor is concerned that a population may contain exceptions,\nthe determination of a sample size sufficient to include at least one\nsuch exception is a characteristic of","options":{"A":"Discovery sampling","B":"Variables sampling","C":"Random sampling","D":"Monetary-unit sampling"},"correctAnswer":"A","topic":"Audit Sampling (Statistical & Non-Statistical)","standard":"PSA 530 - Audit Sampling","rationale":"The tolerable rate of deviation (or failure rate) is the maximum rate set by the auditor for which control risk can still be supported. An auditor estimates this parameter during planning to determine the appropriate sample size necessary to achieve the desired confidence level.","distractorNotes":"Tolerable rate does not determine the sampling approach (statistical vs nonstatistical) nor does it define the population boundaries."},{"id":"AT-300-61","set":"AT-300","num":61,"question":"In a variable sampling plan, an auditor must generally consider each\nof the following except","options":{"A":"variation within the population","B":"acceptable risk of incorrect acceptance","C":"tolerable error","D":"population"},"correctAnswer":"D","topic":"Audit Sampling (Statistical & Non-Statistical)","standard":"PSA 530 - Audit Sampling","rationale":"PSA 530 defines audit sampling as the application of audit procedures to less than 100% of items within a population of audit relevance such that all sampling units have a chance of selection in order to provide the auditor with a reasonable basis on which to draw conclusions about the entire population.","distractorNotes":"Other options misstate the relationship between sampling risk parameters (alpha/beta risk, tolerable misstatement, and sample size)."},{"id":"AT-300-62","set":"AT-300","num":62,"question":"When sampling methods are used in a substantive test, all of the\nfollowing factors must be considered in determining an optimum sample\nsize, except the","options":{"A":"variation in the population","B":"risk levels that the auditor is willing to accept","C":"deviation occurrence rate that the auditor expects to exist in the\nsample","D":"tolerable misstatement"},"correctAnswer":"C","topic":"Audit Sampling (Statistical & Non-Statistical)","standard":"PSA 530 - Audit Sampling","rationale":"PSA 530 defines audit sampling as the application of audit procedures to less than 100% of items within a population of audit relevance such that all sampling units have a chance of selection in order to provide the auditor with a reasonable basis on which to draw conclusions about the entire population.","distractorNotes":"Other options misstate the relationship between sampling risk parameters (alpha/beta risk, tolerable misstatement, and sample size)."},{"id":"AT-300-63","set":"AT-300","num":63,"question":"To gather evidence regarding the balance per bank in a bank\nreconciliation, an auditor would examine all of the following except","options":{"A":"cutoff bank statement","B":"year-end bank statement","C":"bank confirmation","D":"general ledger"},"correctAnswer":"D","topic":"Audit Principles & Evidence","standard":"Philippine Standards on Auditing (PSA)","rationale":"Under auditing standards and professional practice, Option D accurately reflects the applicable requirement. The auditor gathers sufficient appropriate evidence to provide reasonable assurance regarding the relevant management assertions and financial statement disclosures.","distractorNotes":"The other choices are either inconsistent with Philippine auditing standards or represent incorrect procedures for this engagement stage."},{"id":"AT-300-64","set":"AT-300","num":64,"question":"The negative form of accounts receivable confirmation request is\nparticularly useful except when","options":{"A":"control procedures surrounding accounts receivable are considered to\nbe effective","B":"a large number of small balances are involved","C":"the auditor has reason to believe the persons receiving the requests\nare likely to give them consideration","D":"individual account balances are relatively large"},"correctAnswer":"D","topic":"Substantive Testing & Confirmations","standard":"PSA 500, 501 & 505 (Audit Evidence & Confirmations)","rationale":"Under PSA 500 and 505, substantive procedures are designed to detect material misstatements at the assertion level. External co