eeting, all\nof the following may be expected, except","options":{"A":"an oral report of the auditor's major findings","B":"the auditor's rationale for proposed adjustments or additional\ndisclosures","C":"an agreement between the auditor and the client on the changes to be\nmade in the financial statements","D":"the delivery of the management letter."},"correctAnswer":"D","topic":"Audit Principles & Evidence","standard":"Philippine Standards on Auditing (PSA)","rationale":"Under auditing standards and professional practice, Option D accurately reflects the applicable requirement. The auditor gathers sufficient appropriate evidence to provide reasonable assurance regarding the relevant management assertions and financial statement disclosures.","distractorNotes":"The other choices are either inconsistent with Philippine auditing standards or represent incorrect procedures for this engagement stage."},{"id":"AT-300-75","set":"AT-300","num":75,"question":"Analytical procedures enable the auditor to predict the balance or\nquantity of an item under audit. Information to develop this estimate\ncan be obtained from all of the following except","options":{"A":"tracing transactions through the system to determine whether\nprocedures are being applied as prescribed","B":"comparison of financial data with data for comparable prior periods,\nanticipated results (e.g., budgets and forecasts), and similar data for\nthe industry in which the entity operates","C":"study of the relationships of elements of financial data that would\nbe expected to conform to a predictable pattern based upon the entity's\nexperience","D":"study of the relationships of financial data with relevant\nnonfinancial data."},"correctAnswer":"A","topic":"Analytical Procedures","standard":"PSA 520 (Analytical Procedures)","rationale":"Under PSA 520, analytical procedures involve evaluations of financial information made by an analysis of relationships among financial and non-financial data, identifying inconsistencies or unexpected fluctuations.","distractorNotes":"Other options do not align with the purpose, timing, or predictive nature of analytical procedures as defined under PSA 520."},{"id":"AT-300-76","set":"AT-300","num":76,"question":"An auditor should obtain evidential matter relevant to each of the\nfollowing actors concerning third-party litigation against a client\nexcept the","options":{"A":"period in which the underlying cause for legal action occurred","B":"probability of an unfavorable outcome","C":"jurisdiction in which the matter will be resolved","D":"existence of a situation indicating an uncertainty as to the possible\nloss."},"correctAnswer":"C","topic":"Audit Principles & Evidence","standard":"Philippine Standards on Auditing (PSA)","rationale":"Under auditing standards and professional practice, Option C accurately reflects the applicable requirement. The auditor gathers sufficient appropriate evidence to provide reasonable assurance regarding the relevant management assertions and financial statement disclosures.","distractorNotes":"The other choices are either inconsistent with Philippine auditing standards or represent incorrect procedures for this engagement stage."},{"id":"AT-300-77","set":"AT-300","num":77,"question":"When performing an operational audit, the auditor would normally be\nconcerned with all of the following except","options":{"A":"calculation of earnings per share","B":"investigation of budget variance","C":"follow up on inventory shortages","D":"reasons for idle equipment."},"correctAnswer":"A","topic":"Operational & Internal Auditing","standard":"Philippine Framework for Assurance Engagements","rationale":"Operational auditing is a systematic review of an organization's activities in relation to the efficient and effective use of resources, compliance with operational policies, and achievement of organizational objectives.","distractorNotes":"Financial statement audits focus on fair presentation in conformity with PFRS, whereas operational audits focus on economy, efficiency, and effectiveness."},{"id":"AT-300-78","set":"AT-300","num":78,"question":"The benefits of an operational audit generally include all of\nfollowing except","options":{"A":"increased revenue","B":"increased reliability of the financial statements","C":"increases productivity","D":"decreased costs."},"correctAnswer":"B","topic":"Operational & Internal Auditing","standard":"Philippine Framework for Assurance Engagements","rationale":"Operational auditing is a systematic review of an organization's activities in relation to the efficient and effective use of resources, compliance with operational policies, and achievement of organizational objectives.","distractorNotes":"Financial statement audits focus on fair presentation in conformity with PFRS, whereas operational audits focus on economy, efficiency, and effectiveness."},{"id":"AT-300-79","set":"AT-300","num":79,"question":"In a review of interim financial information of a publicly-held\ncompany, the CPA is expected to have an understanding of all of the\nfollowing except the","options":{"A":"industry in which the client operates","B":"client's internal control structure","C":"nature of the entity's organization","D":"entity's accounting practices"},"correctAnswer":"B","topic":"Audit Principles & Evidence","standard":"Philippine Standards on Auditing (PSA)","rationale":"Under auditing standards and professional practice, Option B accurately reflects the applicable requirement. The auditor gathers sufficient appropriate evidence to provide reasonable assurance regarding the relevant management assertions and financial statement disclosures.","distractorNotes":"The other choices are either inconsistent with Philippine auditing standards or represent incorrect procedures for this engagement stage."},{"id":"AT-300-80","set":"AT-300","num":80,"question":"Matters to be agreed in an agreed-upon procedures engagement include\nthe following, except","options":{"A":"stated purpose of the engagement","B":"limitations on distribution of the report of factual findings","C":"anticipated form of the report and the level of assurance to be\nprovided","D":"nature, timing and extent of the specific procedures to be applied"},"correctAnswer":"C","topic":"Agreed-Upon Procedures","standard":"PSRS 4400 - Engagements to Perform Agreed-Upon Procedures","rationale":"In an agreed-upon procedures engagement, the auditor provides a report of factual findings based on specific procedures agreed between the auditor and third parties. No assurance is expressed; instead, users evaluate the procedures and findings themselves. Distribution of the report is restricted to those specified parties who agreed to the procedures.","distractorNotes":"Agreed-upon procedures do not provide positive or negative assurance, and the report cannot be distributed for general public use."},{"id":"AT-300-81","set":"AT-300","num":81,"question":"Reports on compilation engagements should contain the following,\nexcept","options":{"A":"a statement that the engagement was performed in accordance with the\nPSAs applicable to compilation engagements","B":"identification of the financial information indicating that it is\nbased on information provided by the management","C":"a statement that the management is responsible for the financial\ninformation compiled by the accountant","D":"a statement that the accountant does not express an opinion but\nexpresses only a limited assurance on the financial statements"},"correctAnswer":"D","topic":"Compilation Engagements","standard":"PSRS 4410 - Compilation Engagements","rationale":"In a compilation engagement, the accountant applies accounting and financial reporting expertise to assist management in the preparation and presentation of financial information without undertaking to obtain any assurance on the information. The report explicitly states that the statements have not been audited or reviewed and no assurance is expressed.","dist