ssurance regarding the relevant management assertions and financial statement disclosures.","distractorNotes":"The other choices are either inconsistent with Philippine auditing standards or represent incorrect procedures for this engagement stage."},{"id":"AT-300-90","set":"AT-300","num":90,"question":"In a financial statement audit, management is responsible for the\nfollowing except","options":{"A":"the financial statements","B":"for establishing and maintaining internal control","C":"for meeting budget projections","D":"for assuring compliance with laws and regulations."},"correctAnswer":"C","topic":"Audit Principles & Evidence","standard":"Philippine Standards on Auditing (PSA)","rationale":"Under auditing standards and professional practice, Option C accurately reflects the applicable requirement. The auditor gathers sufficient appropriate evidence to provide reasonable assurance regarding the relevant management assertions and financial statement disclosures.","distractorNotes":"The other choices are either inconsistent with Philippine auditing standards or represent incorrect procedures for this engagement stage."},{"id":"AT-300-91","set":"AT-300","num":91,"question":"Engagement risk is, influenced by the risks associated with the\nfollowing, except","options":{"A":"The sufficiency and appropriateness of the evidence likely to be\navailable","B":"The nature and extent of the process used to collect and evaluate\nevidence","C":"The identified user of the assurance engagement report","D":"The nature and form of the subject matter."},"correctAnswer":"C","topic":"Audit Principles & Evidence","standard":"Philippine Standards on Auditing (PSA)","rationale":"Under auditing standards and professional practice, Option C accurately reflects the applicable requirement. The auditor gathers sufficient appropriate evidence to provide reasonable assurance regarding the relevant management assertions and financial statement disclosures.","distractorNotes":"The other choices are either inconsistent with Philippine auditing standards or represent incorrect procedures for this engagement stage."},{"id":"AT-300-92","set":"AT-300","num":92,"question":"Fraudulent companies will prepare financial statements that\nmaterially misleading by doing all of the following except","options":{"A":"understate revenues and assets","B":"understate expenses and liabilities","C":"show financial performance better than industry average","D":"have performance exactly meet announced targets."},"correctAnswer":"A","topic":"Fraud & Error in an Audit","standard":"PSA 240 - The Auditor's Responsibilities Relating to Fraud","rationale":"PSA 240 distinguishes fraud (intentional act by one or more individuals among management, employees, or third parties resulting in a misstatement) from error (unintentional mistake). The primary responsibility for the prevention and detection of fraud rests with management and governance.","distractorNotes":"Other choices misstate auditor responsibilities or fail to recognize the necessity of maintaining professional skepticism."},{"id":"AT-300-93","set":"AT-300","num":93,"question":"Before the practitioners rely on the work of the expert, he should\nobtain sufficient appropriate evidence that the work of the expert is\nadequate by considering the following, except","options":{"A":"the reasonableness and significance of the expert's findings in\nrelation to the objective of the engagement and the conclusion on the\nsubject matter","B":"the professional competence, experience and objectivity of the expert","C":"the findings of the expert support the assertion issued by the party\nresponsible to the subject matter","D":"the reasonableness of the assumptions, methods and source data used\nby the expert"},"correctAnswer":"C","topic":"Audit Principles & Evidence","standard":"Philippine Standards on Auditing (PSA)","rationale":"Under auditing standards and professional practice, Option C accurately reflects the applicable requirement. The auditor gathers sufficient appropriate evidence to provide reasonable assurance regarding the relevant management assertions and financial statement disclosures.","distractorNotes":"The other choices are either inconsistent with Philippine auditing standards or represent incorrect procedures for this engagement stage."},{"id":"AT-300-94","set":"AT-300","num":94,"question":"The auditor should perform the following risk assessment procedures\nto obtain an understanding of the entity and its environment, including\nits internal control, except","options":{"A":"inquiries of management and others within the entity","B":"inquiries of the entity‘s external legal counsel or of valuation\nexperts that the entity has used","C":"analytical procedures","D":"observation arid inspection"},"correctAnswer":"B","topic":"Internal Control & Control Risk","standard":"PSA 315 & PSA 330 (Internal Control & Tests of Controls)","rationale":"Under PSA 315, internal control is designed, implemented, and maintained to provide reasonable assurance regarding reliability of financial reporting, effectiveness and efficiency of operations, and compliance with applicable laws and regulations.","distractorNotes":"Other options reflect management operational targets or clerical procedures that do not directly address internal control objectives."},{"id":"AT-300-95","set":"AT-300","num":95,"question":"When the auditor considers that the service organization activities\nare significantly relevant to the audit and he concludes that it would\nbe efficient to obtain evidence from tests of controls, such evidence\nmay be obtained by, except","options":{"A":"visiting the service organization","B":"performing tests of the client‘s control over activities of the\nservice organization","C":"reviewing the service contract between the client and the service\norganization","D":"obtaining a service organization auditor‘s report that expresses an\nopinion as to the operating effectiveness of the service organization‘s\naccounting and internal control systems for the processing applications\nand internal control systems for the processing applications relevant\nto the audit"},"correctAnswer":"C","topic":"Internal Control & Control Risk","standard":"PSA 315 & PSA 330 (Internal Control & Tests of Controls)","rationale":"Under PSA 315, internal control is designed, implemented, and maintained to provide reasonable assurance regarding reliability of financial reporting, effectiveness and efficiency of operations, and compliance with applicable laws and regulations.","distractorNotes":"Other options reflect management operational targets or clerical procedures that do not directly address internal control objectives."},{"id":"AT-300-96","set":"AT-300","num":96,"question":"An audit program should be sufficiently detailed to provide all of\nthe following except","options":{"A":"Evidential support for the audit opinion","B":"An outline of the work to be done","C":"A record of the work performed","D":"A basis for controlling the audit"},"correctAnswer":"A","topic":"Audit Principles & Evidence","standard":"Philippine Standards on Auditing (PSA)","rationale":"Under auditing standards and professional practice, Option A accurately reflects the applicable requirement. The auditor gathers sufficient appropriate evidence to provide reasonable assurance regarding the relevant management assertions and financial statement disclosures.","distractorNotes":"The other choices are either inconsistent with Philippine auditing standards or represent incorrect procedures for this engagement stage."},{"id":"AT-300-97","set":"AT-300","num":97,"question":"Each of the following might, by itself, form a valid basis for an\nauditor to decide to omit a test except for the","options":{"A":"Difficulty and expense involved in testing a particular item","B":"Assessed level of control risk","C":"Relative risk involved","D":"Relationship between the cost of obtaining evidence and its\nusefulness."},"correctAnswer":"A","topic":"Audit Principles & Evidence","standard":"Philippine Standards on Auditing (PSA)","rationale":"Under auditing standards and professional practice, Option A accurately reflects the applicable requirement. The auditor gathers sufficient appropriate evidence to provide reasonable assurance regarding the relevant management assertions and financial statement disclosures.","distractorNotes":"The other choices are either inconsistent with Philippine auditing standards or represent incorrect procedures for this engagement stage."},{"id":"AT-300-98","set":"AT-300","num":98,"question":"The objectives of the Philippine Accountancy Act of 2004 are the\nfollowing, except","options":{"A":"Standardization and regulation of accounting education","B":"Integration of accountancy profession","C":"Examination for registration of certified public accountants","D":"Supervision, control and regulation of the practice of accountancy."},"correctAnswer":"B","topic":"Regulation of the Profession (RA 9298)","standard":"Republic Act No. 9298 (Philippine Accountancy Act of 2004)","rationale":"The stated objectives of RA 9298 are: (a) the standardization and regulation of accounting education, (b) the examination for registration of certified public accountants, and (c) the supervision, control, and regulation of the practice of accountancy in the Philippines. Integration of the profession is a mandate of the APO (PICPA), not a statutory objective of the Act.","distractorNotes":"Standardization of education, CPA licensure examinations, and supervision/regulation of practice are explicitly codified in Section 3 of RA 9298."},{"id":"AT-300-99","set":"AT-300","num":99,"question":"If the auditor is concerned that a population may contain exceptions,\nthe determination of a sample size sufficient to include at least one\nsuch critical exception is a characteristic of","options":{"A":"Random sampling","B":"Variable sampling","C":"Discovery sampling","D":"Probability-proportional-size sampling"},"correctAnswer":"C","topic":"Audit Sampling (Statistical & Non-Statistical)","standard":"PSA 530 - Audit Sampling","rationale":"The tolerable rate of deviation (or failure rate) is the maximum rate set by the auditor for which control risk can still be supported. An auditor estimates this parameter during planning to determine the appropriate sample size necessary to achieve the desired confidence level.","distractorNotes":"Tolerable rate does not determine the sampling approach (statistical vs nonstatistical) nor does it define the population boundaries."},{"id":"AT-300-100","set":"AT-300","num":100,"question":"The following statements relate to audit evidence, except","options":{"A":"The competence of audit is likely improved by selecting a larger\nsample size or different population items","B":"The evidence obtained from a source outside the client entity is more\npersuasive than that one obtained from within","C":"When a client's accounting and internal controls are effective, the\ninternal evidence obtained is more reliable","D":"The evidence obtained directly by the auditor through physical\nexamination, computation, observation, or confirmation is more competent\nthan the information that is obtained indirectly."},"correctAnswer":"A","topic":"Audit Principles & Evidence","standard":"Philippine Standards on Auditing (PSA)","rationale":"Under auditing standards and professional practice, Option A accurately reflects the applicable requirement. The auditor gathers sufficient appropriate evidence to provide reasonable assurance regarding the relevant management assertions and financial statement disclosures.","distractorNotes":"The other choices are either inconsistent with Philippine auditing standards or represent incorrect procedures for this engagement stage."},{"id":"AT-300-101","set":"AT-300","num":101,"question":"An objective evidence is more reliable than evidence that requires\nconsiderable judgment to determine whether an assertion is correct. 