ect procedures for this engagement stage."},{"id":"AT-300-111","set":"AT-300","num":111,"question":"A former officer, director or employee of the assurance client\nserves as a member of the assurance team. This situation will least\nlikely create","options":{"A":"self-interest threat","B":"self-review threat","C":"intimidation threat","D":"familiarity threat."},"correctAnswer":"C","topic":"Audit Principles & Evidence","standard":"Philippine Standards on Auditing (PSA)","rationale":"Under auditing standards and professional practice, Option C accurately reflects the applicable requirement. The auditor gathers sufficient appropriate evidence to provide reasonable assurance regarding the relevant management assertions and financial statement disclosures.","distractorNotes":"The other choices are either inconsistent with Philippine auditing standards or represent incorrect procedures for this engagement stage."},{"id":"AT-300-112","set":"AT-300","num":112,"question":"Which of the following will least likely impair independence","options":{"A":"An immediate family member of a member of the assurance team is a\ndirector, an officer or an employee of the assurance client in a position\nto exert direct and significant influence over the subject matter of the\nassurance engagement","B":"A member of the assurance team participates in the assurance\nengagement while knowing, or having reason to believe, that he or she\nis to, or may, join the assurance client in the future","C":"A partner or employee of the firm serves as an officer or as a director\non the board of an assurance client","D":"A partner or employee of the firm or a network firm serves as Company\nSecretary for an audit client, the duties and functions undertaken are\nlimited to those of a routine and formal administrative nature as such\nas the -preparation of minutes and. maintenance of statutory returns."},"correctAnswer":"D","topic":"Professional Ethics & Independence","standard":"Philippine Code of Ethics for Professional Accountants","rationale":"The Philippine Code of Ethics establishes five fundamental principles (Integrity, Objectivity, Professional Competence & Due Care, Confidentiality, and Professional Behavior) and a conceptual framework requiring CPAs to identify, evaluate, and address threats to compliance through appropriate safeguards.","distractorNotes":"Other choices either misclassify the threat category or propose actions that do not satisfy required ethical safeguards."},{"id":"AT-300-113","set":"AT-300","num":113,"question":"Examples of circumstances that may create familiarity threat least\nlikely include","options":{"A":"a member of the assurance team having an immediate family member or\nclose family member who is a director or officer of the assurance client","B":"a member of the assurance team having an immediate family member or\nclose family member who, as an employee of the assurance client, is in\na position to exert direct and significant influence over the subject\nmatter of the assurance engagement","C":"a former partner of the firm being a director, officer of the assurance\nclient or an employee in a position to exert direct and significant\ninfluence over the subject matter of the assurance engagement","D":"dealing in, or being a promoter of, share or other securities in an\nassurance client."},"correctAnswer":"D","topic":"Professional Ethics & Independence","standard":"Philippine Code of Ethics for Professional Accountants","rationale":"The Philippine Code of Ethics establishes five fundamental principles (Integrity, Objectivity, Professional Competence & Due Care, Confidentiality, and Professional Behavior) and a conceptual framework requiring CPAs to identify, evaluate, and address threats to compliance through appropriate safeguards.","distractorNotes":"Other choices either misclassify the threat category or propose actions that do not satisfy required ethical safeguards."},{"id":"AT-300-114","set":"AT-300","num":114,"question":"When a member of the assurance team knows that his or her close\nfamily member has a direct financial interest or a material indirect\nfinancial interest in the assurance client, a self-interest threat may\nbe created. Safeguards least likely include","options":{"A":"The close family member disposing of all or a sufficient portion of\nthe financial interest at the earliest practical date","B":"Discussing the matter with those charged with governance, such as the\naudit committee","C":"Involving a professional accountant who took part in the assurance\nengagement to review the work done by the member of the assurance team\nwith the close family relationship or otherwise advice as necessary","D":"Removing the individual from the assurance engagement."},"correctAnswer":"C","topic":"Professional Ethics & Independence","standard":"Philippine Code of Ethics for Professional Accountants","rationale":"The Philippine Code of Ethics establishes five fundamental principles (Integrity, Objectivity, Professional Competence & Due Care, Confidentiality, and Professional Behavior) and a conceptual framework requiring CPAs to identify, evaluate, and address threats to compliance through appropriate safeguards.","distractorNotes":"Other choices either misclassify the threat category or propose actions that do not satisfy required ethical safeguards."},{"id":"AT-300-115","set":"AT-300","num":115,"question":"Examples of close business relationships that may create self-\ninterest and intimidation threat least likely include","options":{"A":"Having a material financial interest in director, officer or assurance\nclient or a controlling owner, director, officer or other individual who\nperforms senior managerial functions for that client","B":"Arrangements to combine one or more services or products of the firm\nwith one or more sees or products of the assurance client and to market\nthe package with reference to both parties","C":"Distribution or marketing arrangements under which the firm acts as\na distributor or marketer of the assurance client's products or services,\nor the assurance client acts as the distributor or marketer of the\nproducts or services of the firm","D":"The purchase of goods and services from an assurance client by the\nfirm (or from an audit client by a network firm) or a member of the\nassurance team, provided the transaction is in the normal course of\nbusiness and on an arm's length basis."},"correctAnswer":"D","topic":"Professional Ethics & Independence","standard":"Philippine Code of Ethics for Professional Accountants","rationale":"The Philippine Code of Ethics establishes five fundamental principles (Integrity, Objectivity, Professional Competence & Due Care, Confidentiality, and Professional Behavior) and a conceptual framework requiring CPAs to identify, evaluate, and address threats to compliance through appropriate safeguards.","distractorNotes":"Other choices either misclassify the threat category or propose actions that do not satisfy required ethical safeguards."},{"id":"AT-300-116","set":"AT-300","num":116,"question":"If a member of the assurance team, partner or former partner of the\nfirm has joined the assurance client, the significance of the self-\ninterest, familiarity or intimidation threats created is least likely\naffected by","options":{"A":"The position the individual has taken at the assurance client","B":"The amount of any involvement the individual will have with the member\nof the assurance team","C":"The length of time that the individual was a member of the assurance\nteam or firm","D":"The former position of the individual within the assurance team or\nfirm."},"correctAnswer":"C","topic":"Professional Ethics & Independence","standard":"Philippine Code of Ethics for Professional Accountants","rationale":"The Philippine Code of Ethics establishes five fundamental principles (Integrity, Objectivity, Professional Competence & Due Care, Confidentiality, and Professional Behavior) and a conceptual framework requiring CPAs to identify, evaluate, and address threats to compliance through appropriate safeguards.","distractorNotes":"Other choices either misclassify the threat category or propose actions that do not satisfy required ethical safeguards."},{"id":"AT-300-117","set":"AT-300","num":117,"question":"Using the same senior personnel on an assurance engagement over a\nlong period of time may create a familiarity threat. The significance\nof the threat will least likely depend upon","options":{"A":"The length of time that the individual has been a member of the\nassurance team","B":"The role of the individual on the assurance team","C":"The structure of the client","D":"The nature of the assurance engagement."},"correctAnswer":"C","topic":"Professional Ethics & Independence","standard":"Philippine Code of Ethics for Professional Accountants","rationale":"The Philippine Code of Ethics establishes five fundamental principles (Integrity, Objectivity, Professional Competence & Due Care, Confidentiality, and Professional Behavior) and a conceptual framework requiring CPAs to identify, evaluate, and address threats to compliance through appropriate safeguards.","distractorNotes":"Other choices either misclassify the threat category or propose actions that do not satisfy required ethical safeguards."},{"id":"AT-300-118","set":"AT-300","num":118,"question":"The firm, or a network firm, may provide an audit client that is\nnot a listed entity with accounting and bookkeeping services, including\npayroll services, of a routine or mechanical nature, provided any self-\nreview threat created is reduced to an acceptable level. Examples of\nsuch services least likely include","options":{"A":"Recording transactions for which the audit client has determined or\napproved the appropriate account classification","B":"Posting coded transactions to the audit client's general ledger","C":"Preparing financial statements based on information in the trial\nbalance","D":"Determining and posting journal entries without obtaining the approval\nof the audit client."},"correctAnswer":"D","topic":"Audit Principles & Evidence","standard":"Philippine Standards on Auditing (PSA)","rationale":"Under auditing standards and professional practice, Option D accurately reflects the applicable requirement. The auditor gathers sufficient appropriate evidence to provide reasonable assurance regarding the relevant management assertions and financial statement disclosures.","distractorNotes":"The other choices are either inconsistent with Philippine auditing standards or represent incorrect procedures for this engagement stage."},{"id":"AT-300-119","set":"AT-300","num":119,"question":"Which of the following may least likely create a self-review threat","options":{"A":"The lending of staff by a firm to the financial Statement audit\nclient","B":"The firm provides internal audit services to the financial statement\naudit client","C":"The firm renders litigation support services to the financial\nstatement audit","D":"Recruitment of senior manager for the financial statement audit\nclient."},"correctAnswer":"D","topic":"Professional Ethics & Independence","standard":"Philippine Code of Ethics for Professional Accountants","rationale":"The Philippine Code of Ethics establishes five fundamental principles (Integrity, Objectivity, Professional Competence & Due Care, Confidentiality, and Professional Behavior) and a conceptual framework requiring CPAs to identify, evaluate, and address threats to compliance through appropriate safeguards.","distractorNotes":"Other choices either misclassify the threat category or propose actions that do not satisfy required ethical safeguards."},{"id":"AT-300-120","set":"AT-300","num":120,"question":"Which of the following is least likely considered to create a threat\nto independence","options":{"A":"The provision of services by a firm or network firm to an audit client\nwhich involve either the design or the implementation of financial\ninformation technology systems that are used to generate information\nforming part of a client's financial statements","B":"The provision of services in connection with the assessment, design\nand implementation of internal accounting controls and risk management\ncontrols","C":"The lending of staff by a firm, or network firm, to an. audit client\nwhen the individual is in a position to influence the preparation of a\nclient's accounts or financial statements","D":"The provision of litigation support services to an audit client which\ninclude the estimation of the possible outcome and thereby affects the\namounts or disclosures to be reflected in the financial statements."},"correctAnswer":"B","topic":"Professional Ethics & Independence","standard":"Philippine Code of Ethics for Professional Accountants","rationale":"The Philippine Code of Ethics establishes five fundamental principles (Integrity, Objectivity, Professional Competence & Due Care, Confidentiality, and Professional Behavior) and a conceptual framework requiring CPAs to identify, evaluate, and address threats to compliance through appropriate safeguards.","distractorNotes":"Other choices either misclassify the threat category or propose actions that do not satisfy required ethical safeguards."},{"id":"AT-300-121","set":"AT-300","num":121,"question":"Which of the following is least likely to create a threat to\nindependence","options":{"A":"The fees generated by the assurance client represent a large\nproportion of the revenue of an individual partner","B":"The firm charges a contingent fee to an assurance client","C":"Accepting gifts or hospitality, the value of which is clearly\ninsignificant, from an assurance client","D":"When litigation takes place, or appears likely, between the firm or\na member of the assurance team and the assurance client."},"correctAnswer":"C","topic":"Professional Ethics & Independence","standard":"Philippine Code of Ethics for Professional Accountants","rationale":"The Philippine Code of Ethics establishes five fundamental principles (Integrity, Objectivity, Professional Competence & Due Care, Confidentiality, and Professional Behavior) and a conceptual framework requiring CPAs to identify, evaluate, and address threats to compliance through appropriate safeguards.","distractorNotes":"Other choices either misclassify the threat category or propose actions that do not satisfy required ethical safeguards."},{"id":"AT-300-122","set":"AT-300","num":122,"question":"When litigation takes place between the firm and the assurance\nclient, the firm and the client management may be placed in adversarial\npositions and the firm may face a self-interestthreat. Which of the\nfollowing is least likely a factor in determining the Significance of\nthe threat created by this litigation","options":{"A":"The nature of the-assurance engagement","B":"Whether the litigation relates to a prior assurance engagement","C":"The materiality of the amount involved on litigation","D":"The likelihood of the firm winning the litigation."},"correctAnswer":"D","topic":"Professional Ethics & Independence","standard":"Philippine Code of Ethics for Professional Accountants","rationale":"The Philippine Code of Ethics establishes five fundamental principles (Integrity, Objectivity, Professional Competence & Due Care, Confidentiality, and Professional Behavior) and a conceptual framework requiring CPAs to identify, evaluate, and address threats to compliance through appropriate safeguards.","distractorNotes":"Other choices either misclassify the threat category or propose actions that do not satisfy required ethical safeguards."},{"id":"AT-300-123","set":"AT-300","num":123,"question":"Which of the following threats to independence is least likely\nconsidered a result of the firm's service of recruiting senior managers\nfor an assurance client","options":{"A":"Self-interest threat","B":"Familiarity threat","C":"Intimidation threat","D":"Self-review threat"},"correctAnswer":"D","topic":"Professional Ethics & Independence","standard":"Philippine Code of Ethics for Professional Accountants","rationale":"The Philippine Code of Ethics establishes five fundamental principles (Integrity, Objectivity, Professional Competence & Due Care, Confidentiality, and Professional Behavior) and a conceptual framework requiring CPAs to identify, evaluate, and address threats to compliance through appropriate safeguards.","distractorNotes":"Other choices either misclassify the threat category or propose actions that do not satisfy required ethical safeguards."},{"id":"AT-300-124","set":"AT-300","num":124,"question":"Which of the following is least likely an application of maintaining\nan attitude of professional skepticism","options":{"A":"The auditor does not consider representations from management as\nsubstitute for obtaining sufficient appropriate audit evidence to be\nable to draw reasonable conclusions on which to base the audit opinion","B":"In planning and performing an audit, the auditor assumes that\nmanagement is dishonest","C":"The auditor is alert to audit evidence that contradicts or brings\ninto question the reliability of documents or management\nrepresentations","D":"The auditor makes a critical assessment, with a questioning mind, of\nthe validity of audit evidence obtained."},"correctAnswer":"B","topic":"Audit Principles & Evidence","standard":"Philippine Standards on Auditing (PSA)","rationale":"Under auditing standards and professional practice, Option B accurately reflects the applicable requirement. The auditor gathers sufficient appropriate evidence to provide reasonable assurance regarding the relevant management assertions and financial statement disclosures.","distractorNotes":"The other choices are either inconsistent with Philippine auditing standards or represent incorrect procedures for this engagement stage."},{"id":"AT-300-125","set":"AT-300","num":125,"question":"PSA 800, “The Independent Auditor‘s Report on Special Purpose Audit\nEngagements” least likely applies to auditor‘s report issued as a result\nof an audit of","options":{"A":"compliance to royalty engagement","B":"a component of a complete set of general purpose financial statements\nwherein the opinion of the auditor is unqualified","C":"complete set of general purpose financial statements where the auditor\nis prohibited from observing the inventory count","D":"summarized financial statements"},"correctAnswer":"C","topic":"Audit Principles & Evidence","standard":"Philippine Standards on Auditing (PSA)","rationale":"Under auditing standards and professional practice, Option C accurately reflects the applicable requirement. The auditor gathers sufficient appropriate evidence to provide reasonable assurance regarding the relevant management assertions and financial statement disclosures.","distractorNotes":"The other choices are either inconsistent with Philippine auditing standards or represent incorrect procedures for this engagement stage."},{"id":"AT-300-126","set":"AT-300","num":126,"question":"Which of the following is least likely considered by the auditor\nwhen he has to evaluate the fair presentation of the financial\nstatements","options":{"A":"Whether the financial statements, after any adjustments made by the\nmanagement as a result of audit process, are consistent with the\nauditor‘s understanding of the entity and its environment","B":"Whether the financial statements, including the disclosures\nfaithfully represent the underlying transaction and events in a manner\nthat gives a true and fair view of, in all material respects, the\ninformation conveyed in the financial statements within the context of\nthe financial reporting framework","C":"Whether the results of analytical procedures performed at or near the\nend of the audit help to corroborate conclusion formed during the audit","D":"Whether the financial statements are approved by the client‘s board\nof directors"},"correctAnswer":"D","topic":"Audit Principles & Evidence","standard":"Philippine Standards on Auditing (PSA)","rationale":"Under auditing standards and professional practice, Option D accurately reflects the applicable requirement. The auditor gathers sufficient appropriate evidence to provide reasonable assurance regarding the relevant management assertions and financial statement disclosures.","distractorNotes":"The other choices are either inconsistent with Philippine auditing standards or represent incorrect procedures for this engagement stage."},{"id":"AT-300-127","set":"AT-300","num":127,"question":"Which of the following situations, the effect of which is\nsignificant, least likely require a decision of whether to issue a\nqualified or adverse opinion","options":{"A":"Any disagreement with entity management regarding the acceptability\nof the accounting policies selected by the management","B":"Limitation on the scope of the auditor's work","C":"Inadequate disclosures of financial information","D":"Unjustified changes in accounting policies"},"correctAnswer":"B","topic":"Audit Opinions & Reports","standard":"PSA 700 / 705 / 706 (Auditor's Reports & Modifications)","rationale":"An adverse opinion is expressed under PSA 705 when the auditor, having obtained sufficient appropriate audit evidence, concludes that misstatements are both material and pervasive to the financial statements (such as fundamental non-conformity with PFRS).","distractorNotes":"Scope limitations lead to qualified or disclaimer of opinions, not adverse opinions."},{"id":"AT-300-128","set":"AT-300","num":128,"question":"Which of the following circumstances least likely result to either\na qualified opinion or an auditor disclaiming his opinion","options":{"A":"The auditor is unable to carry out an audit procedure believed to be\ndesirable; the auditor carried out alternative audit procedures to\nsupport the management's assertion","B":"The auditor believed the client's accounting records are inadequate","C":"A client-imposed scope limitation with respect to the audit of\ninventory","D":"Circumstances did not permit the auditor to perform certain required\naudit procedure."},"correctAnswer":"A","topic":"Audit Opinions & Reports","standard":"PSA 700 / 705 / 706 (Auditor's Reports & Modifications)","rationale":"Under PSA 705, a disclaimer of opinion is issued when the auditor is unable to obtain sufficient appropriate audit evidence (severe scope limitation) and concludes that the possible undetected misstatements could be both material and pervasive, or in extremely rare situations involving multiple pervasive uncertainties.","distractorNotes":"Material departures from GAAP/PFRS result in qualified or adverse opinions, not disclaimers."},{"id":"AT-300-129","set":"AT-300","num":129,"question":"Which of the following least likely requires an expression of\nunqualified opinion with modified wordings or an emphasis of the matter\nparagraph","options":{"A":"The financial statements of prior period, which are presented for\ncomparative purposes, were audited by as 'Gillet CPAs","B":"The auditors have substantial doubt about the ability of the entity\nto continue as a going concern","C":"The entity changed the measurement of certain significant transaction\nfrom one GAAP to another GAAP","D":"The auditors failed to observe physical inventory count. However, the\nauditor was satisfied that the inventory amount was fairly presented by\ndoing alternative audit procedures."},"correctAnswer":"D","topic":"Audit Opinions & Reports","standard":"PSA 700 / 705 / 706 (Auditor's Reports & Modifications)","rationale":"Under PSA 700 and PSA 705, the auditor evaluates whether the financial statements are prepared, in all material respects, in accordance with the applicable financial reporting framework, determining whether an unmodified, qualified, adverse, or disclaimer of opinion is required.","distractorNotes":"The other choices confuse the circumstances of GAAP departure (qualified/adverse) with scope restrictions (qualified/disclaimer) or emphasis paragraphs."},{"id":"AT-300-130","set":"AT-300","num":130,"question":"In which of the following conditions is an unqualified audit opinion\nleast likely issued","options":{"A":"The auditor believes that a substantial doubt about the entity's\nability to continue as a going concern exists","B":"The auditor believes that inventory is valued at market values that\naccurately reflect market conditions and materially exceed cost","C":"The audit is conducted with no circumstance or imposed scope\nlimitation","D":"PFRS are not consistently applied from year to year"},"correctAnswer":"B","topic":"Audit Opinions & Reports","standard":"PSA 700 / 705 / 706 (Auditor's Reports & Modifications)","rationale":"Under PSA 700 and PSA 705, the auditor evaluates whether the financial statements are prepared, in all material respects, in accordance with the applicable financial reporting framework, determining whether an unmodified, qualified, adverse, or disclaimer of opinion is required.","distractorNotes":"The other choices confuse the circumstances of GAAP departure (qualified/adverse) with scope restrictions (qualified/disclaimer) or emphasis paragraphs."},{"id":"AT-300-131","set":"AT-300","num":131,"question":"Which of the following subsequent events will be least likely to\nresult to an adjustment on the financial statements","options":{"A":"Culmination of events affecting the realization value of accounts\nreceivable owned as of the balance sheet date","B":"Culmination of events affecting the realization of inventories owned\nas of the balance sheet date","C":"Material changes in the settlement of liabilities which were estimated\nas of the balance sheet date","D":"Material changes in the quoted market prices of listed investment\nsecurities since the balance sheet date."},"correctAnswer":"D","topic":"Subsequent Events & Discovery of Facts","standard":"PSA 560 - Subsequent Events","rationale":"PSA 560 classifies subsequent events into adjusting events (Type 1, reflecting pre-existing conditions) and non-adjusting events (Type 2, reflecting new post-period events requiring disclosure).","distractorNotes":"Other options confuse conditions existing at the balance sheet date with post-balance sheet occurrences."},{"id":"AT-300-132","set":"AT-300","num":132,"question":"Which of the following statement would least likely appear in an\nauditor‘s engagement letter","options":{"A":"The fees for our serviced are based on our regular per diem rates,\nplus travel and other out-of-pocket expenses","B":"During the course of our audit, we may observe opportunities for\neconomy in, or improved controls over, your operations","C":"Our engagement is subject to the risk that material errors, fraud,\nand defalcations, if they exist, will not be detected","D":"After performing our preliminary analytical procedures we will discuss\nwith you the other procedures we consider necessary to complete the\nengagement"},"correctAnswer":"D","topic":"Audit Principles & Evidence","standard":"Philippine Standards on Auditing (PSA)","rationale":"Under auditing standards and professional practice, Option D accurately reflects the applicable requirement. The auditor gathers sufficient appropriate evidence to provide reasonable assurance regarding the relevant management assertions and financial statement disclosures.","distractorNotes":"The other choices are either inconsistent with Philippine auditing standards or represent incorrect procedures for this engagement stage."},{"id":"AT-300-133","set":"AT-300","num":133,"question":"Which of the following is least likely included in an auditor‘s\ninquiry of management while obtaining information to identify the risks\nof material misstatement due to fraud","options":{"A":"Are financial reporting operations controlled by and limited to one\nlocation","B":"Does it have knowledge of fraud or suspect or fraud","C":"Does it have programs to mitigate fraud risks","D":"Has it reported to the audit committee the nature of the company‘s\ninternal control"},"correctAnswer":"A","topic":"Fraud & Error in an Audit","standard":"PSA 240 - The Auditor's Responsibilities Relating to Fraud","rationale":"PSA 240 distinguishes fraud (intentional act by one or more individuals among management, employees, or third parties resulting in a misstatement) from error (unintentional mistake). The primary responsibility for the prevention and detection of fraud rests with management and governance.","distractorNotes":"Other choices misstate auditor responsibilities or fail to recognize the necessity of maintaining professional skepticism."},{"id":"AT-300-134","set":"AT-300","num":134,"question":"The auditor should determine overall responses to address the risks\nof material misstatement at the financial statement level. Such responses\nleast likely include","options":{"A":"Emphasizing to the audit team the need to maintain professional\nskepticism in gathering and evaluating audit evidence","B":"Assigning more experienced staff or those with special skills or using\nexperts","C":"Incorporating additional elements of unpredictability in the\nselection of further audit procedures to be performed","D":"Performing substantive procedures at an interim date instead of at\nperiod end"},"correctAnswer":"D","topic":"Audit Principles & Evidence","standard":"Philippine Standards on Auditing (PSA)","rationale":"Under auditing standards and professional practice, Option D accurately reflects the applicable requirement. The auditor gathers sufficient appropriate evidence to provide reasonable assurance regarding the relevant management assertions and financial statement disclosures.","distractorNotes":"The other choices are either inconsistent with Philippine auditing standards or represent incorrect procedures for this engagement stage."},{"id":"AT-300-135","set":"AT-300","num":135,"question":"Which of the following is least likely considered a financial\nstatement audit risk factor","options":{"A":"Management operating and financing decisions are dominated by top\nmanagement","B":"A new client with no prior audit history","C":"Rate of change in the entity‘s industry is rapid","D":"Profitability of the entity relative to its industry is inconsistent"},"correctAnswer":"A","topic":"Audit Principles & Evidence","standard":"Philippine Standards on Auditing (PSA)","rationale":"Under auditing standards and professional practice, Option A accurately reflects the applicable requirement. The auditor gathers sufficient appropriate evidence to provide reasonable assurance regarding the relevant management assertions and financial statement disclosures.","distractorNotes":"The other choices are either inconsistent with Philippine auditing standards or represent incorrect procedures for this engagement stage."},{"id":"AT-300-136","set":"AT-300","num":136,"question":"Which of the following will an auditor least likely discuss with\nthe former auditors of a potential client prior to acceptance of an audit\nengagement","options":{"A":"Integrity of the management","B":"Fees charges for the services","C":"Disagreements between the predecessor auditor and the management\nregarding accounting principles","D":"Reasons for changing audit firms"},"correctAnswer":"B","topic":"Audit Principles & Evidence","standard":"Philippine Standards on Auditing (PSA)","rationale":"Under auditing standards and professional practice, Option B accurately reflects the applicable requirement. The auditor gathers sufficient appropriate evidence to provide reasonable assurance regarding the relevant management assertions and financial statement disclosures.","distractorNotes":"The other choices are either inconsistent with Philippine auditing standards or represent incorrect procedures for this engagement stage."},{"id":"AT-300-137","set":"AT-300","num":137,"question":"Which of the following does an auditor least likely perform in\nassessing audit risk","options":{"A":"Gather audit evidence in sup