express\nan adverse opinion","options":{"A":"The chief executive officer refuses the auditor's access to minutes\nof the board of directors' meetings","B":"Tests of controls show that the client's internal control is so\nineffective that it cannot be relied upon","C":"The financial statements are not in conformity with the FRSC\nStatements regarding the capitalization of leases","D":"Information comes to the auditor's attention than raises substantial\ndoubt about the client's ability to continue as a going concern."},"correctAnswer":"C","topic":"Audit Opinions & Reports","standard":"PSA 700 / 705 / 706 (Auditor's Reports & Modifications)","rationale":"An adverse opinion is expressed under PSA 705 when the auditor, having obtained sufficient appropriate audit evidence, concludes that misstatements are both material and pervasive to the financial statements (such as fundamental non-conformity with PFRS).","distractorNotes":"Scope limitations lead to qualified or disclaimer of opinions, not adverse opinions."},{"id":"AT-300-206","set":"AT-300","num":206,"question":"The adverse effects of events causing an auditor to believe there\nis substantial doubt about an entity's ability to continue as a going\nconcern would most likely be mitigated by evidence relating to the","options":{"A":"ability to expand operations into new product lines in the future","B":"feasibility of plans to purchase leased equipment at less than market\nvalue","C":"marketability of assets that management plans to sell","D":"committed arrangements to convert preferred stock to long-term debt."},"correctAnswer":"C","topic":"Going Concern Assessment","standard":"PSA 570 (Revised) - Going Concern","rationale":"Under PSA 570, the auditor must assess whether there are material events or conditions that may cast significant doubt on the entity's ability to continue as a going concern, and evaluate management's mitigation strategies.","distractorNotes":"Other options represent unrelated business operational matters or factors that exacerbate rather than alleviate solvency concerns."},{"id":"AT-300-207","set":"AT-300","num":207,"question":"Which of the following events occurring after the issuance of an\nauditor's report most likely would cause die auditor to make further\ninquiries about the previously issued financial statements","options":{"A":"A technological development that could affect the entity's future\nability to continue as a going concern","B":"The discovery of information regarding a contingency that existed\nbefore the financial statements were issued","C":"The entity's sale of a subsidiary that accounts for 30 per cent of\nthe entity's consolidated sales","D":"The final resolution of a lawsuit that was fully explained in a\nfootnote."},"correctAnswer":"B","topic":"Subsequent Events & Discovery of Facts","standard":"PSA 560 - Subsequent Events","rationale":"Under PSA 560, after the financial statements are issued, the auditor has no general obligation to perform further procedures. However, if the auditor becomes aware of a fact that existed at the date of the report which, if known, might have altered the report (such as discovery of an existing contingency), the auditor must discuss the matter with management and determine whether the statements need revision.","distractorNotes":"Events occurring after issuance that relate to new conditions (such as subsequent technological changes or new subsidiary sales) do not require reopening prior statements."},{"id":"AT-300-208","set":"AT-300","num":208,"question":"A principal auditor decides not to refer to the audit by another\nCPA who audited a subsidiary of the principal auditor's client. After\nmaking inquiries about the other CPA's professional reputation and\nindependence, the principal auditor most likely would","options":{"A":"add an explanatory paragraph to the auditor's report indicating that\nthe subsidiary's financial statements are not material to the\nconsolidated financial statements","B":"docum